61218:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '78,165p' docs/blueprint_style_guide.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Notation Consistency\nNotation must be **internally consistent** across the entire blueprint and **close to what the Lean code expresses**:\n\n- **Indices**: 0-indexed where matching `Fin n` in Lean\n- **Finite fields / alphabets**: use standard notation for the alphabet $\\Sigma$ and finite fields $\\mathbb{F}_q$\n- **Measurements**: use standard quantum measurement notation (projective measurements, POVMs)\n- **Strategies**: quantum strategies for nonlocal games should use standard notation from the literature\n- **Expansion**: use standard notation for graph expansion parameters\n- **Macros** (in `macros/common.tex`): define project-specific macros and use them consistently\n\nThe original reference controls notation whenever it gives a usable convention.\nIf the blueprint needs a different symbol, index convention, or name in order to\nmatch Lean, introduce it explicitly and relate it to the paper notation in the\nsame paragraph.  Do not let a `\\lean{...}` tag, a Lean namespace, or a file name\nbe the only explanation of a changed term.\n\nWhen a public Lean declaration is a formalization-only auxiliary lemma, the\nblueprint should say so before listing the declaration.  The surrounding prose\nshould identify the paper theorem, equation, or construction that the auxiliary\nlemma supports, and should avoid making the auxiliary name look like a named\nresult from the source article.\n\n## What NOT to Put in the Blueprint\n- **Lean identifier names in math text.** Write \"the self-improvement lemma\", never \"the `selfImprovement` step\". The `\\lean{LDT.selfImprovement}` tag handles the linking.\n- **Implementation details.** Don't say \"bundled as an element of the Euclidean space\" or \"using `EuclideanSpace.equiv`\". Describe the mathematical object.\n- **Ad-hoc notation.** Don't invent superscripts or subscripts to distinguish from existing notation. Use standard conventions.\n- **Function-call syntax.** Use mathematical notation, not programming notation.\n- **Redundant definitions.** If two blueprint definitions describe the same mathematical object, consolidate them. Each definition should introduce genuinely new mathematical content.\n- **Lean namespace prefixes in prose.** Don't write \"the `LDT.PastingLemma`\" — write \"the pasting lemma\".\n\n## Banned AI/Software Language (enforced in both blueprint AND Lean code)\nThe blueprint reads as a **mathematical document**, not software documentation. The following patterns are banned in ALL reader-facing text (section titles, theorem names, proof sketches, remarks, chapter preambles) and in ALL Lean docstrings, comments, and section names:\n\n### Banned terms → replacements:\n| Banned | Use instead |\n|--------|------------|\n| \"Assembly\" (as section/chapter title) | \"Proof of [theorem]\", \"Construction\", \"Composition\" |\n| \"Pipeline\" | \"reduction\", \"construction\", \"proof chain\" |\n| \"Bridge\" (as noun for a connection) | \"connection\", name by mathematical content |\n| \"Handoff\" / \"hand off\" | \"transition\", \"continuation\", or drop entirely |\n| \"In this blueprint\" / \"Within this blueprint\" | \"here\", \"in what follows\", or omit |\n| \"Honest\" (as adjective for rigorous) | \"exact\", \"faithful\", \"unconditional\", \"complete\" |\n| \"Glue layer\" | \"intermediate construction\", \"connecting results\" |\n| \"Re-export\" / \"reexport\" | \"provides\", \"re-states\" |\n| \"Wiring\" / \"wire up\" | \"connecting\", \"composing\", \"combining\" |\n| \"Package\" (as noun for a data bundle) | \"form\", \"data\", \"structure\" |\n| \"Sorry-free\" | acceptable only in Lean-specific technical context |\n\n### Additional rules:\n- **Section names in Lean**: use mathematical terms (`section QuantumSoundness`, `section SelfImprovement`, `section PastingLemma`), not organizational terms (`section Assembly`, `section Pipeline`).\n- **Internal LaTeX labels** (e.g., `\\label{ch:soundness}`) are not reader-facing and need not be renamed if doing so would break cross-references. But NEW labels should follow the standard.\n- **Definitions that are actually theorems**: if a statement asserts a mathematical fact, it must be `\\begin{theorem}`, not `\\begin{definition}`. Definitions introduce new objects; theorems prove properties.\n\n## `\\uses` Dependency Guidelines\n- **Statement `\\uses`**: list only what's needed to *state* the result (typically definitions of the objects involved). Keep minimal — transitive deps are automatic.\n- **Proof `\\uses`**: list only what the proof *actually calls*. Don't list definitions unless the proof unfolds that definition.\n- **Never self-reference**: a proof's `\\uses` must NOT include the label of the theorem it proves.\n\n## Recording Formalization-Only Lemmas\nSome Lean lemmas are genuine proof infrastructure rather than named statements\nfrom the original papers. These must be traceable without making the blueprint\nlook as if the paper stated them.\n\n- If a declaration directly formalizes a paper or blueprint statement, record\n  it as usual with `\\lean{...}` on that statement and cite the relevant source.\n- If a public declaration is an auxiliary lemma introduced only for the Lean\n  proof, make that status explicit. Its Lean docstring should say that it is a\n  formalization-only auxiliary lemma and should name the nearby paper equation,\n  anchored paper passage, or blueprint label it supports. Blueprint line\n  ranges are derived review context, not stored docstring citations; resolve a\n  label with `python3 scripts/blueprint_citations.py resolve <label>`.\n- If such an auxiliary declaration is important enough to be referenced from\n  the blueprint, give it a clearly subordinate blueprint node, for example\n  `[Formalization support for ...]`, and state in the first sentence that the\n  lemma is not a named statement of the paper but isolates a step used in the\n  Lean proof.\n- Connect auxiliary nodes to the corresponding result from the paper with `\\uses{...}` from\n  the proof that actually calls them. Do not add them only to silence a sync\n  warning; the dependency edge should explain where the auxiliary result is\n  used.\n- Do not mark an auxiliary node with proof-level `\\leanok` unless its Lean proof\n  is complete and `#print axioms` has no `sorryAx`. Statement-level `\\leanok`\n  is acceptable only when the Lean statement is intentionally recorded but the\n  proof remains tracked as incomplete.\n\n## Blueprint Structure\n- `content.tex` is a router: `\\input{chapter/ch01_intro}` etc.\n- Each chapter is a separate file in `chapter/`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
61223:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '436,690p' /home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr496/3a23003a8ba216b9a79418f9bd23e0cca36453df/diff.patch\"","aggregated_output":"+            pastedMeasurement (fun g => (G₁ q.1.1).effect g)\n+              (fun g => (G₂ q.1.1).effect g) g₁ g₂ else 0) := by\n+    simp only [G₁', G₂', Measurement.rightPlacement_effect, pastedMeasurement,\n+      heteroKron_mul, one_mul]\n+    simp_rw [heteroKron_finset_sum_right]\n+    apply Finset.sum_congr rfl\n+    intro g₁ _\n+    apply Finset.sum_congr rfl\n+    intro g₂ _\n+    split_ifs <;> simp [heteroKron, Matrix.kronecker]\n+  simp_rw [hpaste] at hpadded\n+  simpa only [A', ψ', Measurement.leftPlacement_effect,\n+    Pasting.consistencyDefect_productPaddedState] using hpadded\n+\n+end MIPStarRE.QPBT\ndiff --git a/audits/2026-09-09-qpbt-heterogeneous-pasting.md b/audits/2026-09-09-qpbt-heterogeneous-pasting.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..b9b40c67\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/audits/2026-09-09-qpbt-heterogeneous-pasting.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@\n+# Heterogeneous Pasting: Statement Integrity\n+\n+This audit concerns issue #495 and paper `lem:pasting`,\n+`references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:504-525`.\n+Its intended application is `lem:qld-xz-lines`,\n+`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:882-963`.\n+\n+## Source Theorem\n+\n+The paper assumes a probability distribution on triples `(x,y1,y2)`, two\n+codeword evaluation maps, measurement families `G1,G2`, and a joint projective\n+measurement family `A`. The family `G2` is projective. Distinct second\n+codewords collide with conditional probability at most `eta`, conditioned on\n+each pair `(x,y1)` of positive marginal probability. Both marginals of `A`\n+are consistent with the corresponding evaluated codeword family to error\n+`delta`; the joint family `A` also satisfies the printed self-consistency\n+comparison. The state is normalized and the error parameters are nonnegative.\n+\n+The conclusion compares `A` with the evaluated sandwich `G2 G1 G2`, with one\n+universal polynomial error function of `(eta,delta)`. The existing additive\n+interpretation of this polynomial error is unchanged; its justification is\n+`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_pasting-product-error.tex`, issue #201.\n+\n+The public Lean statement `exists_pasting_error` is unchanged. All paper\n+hypotheses, including the third comparison, and the conclusion are retained.\n+Its proof is now a corollary of an extracted two-comparison proof.\n+The declaration `pastedMeasurement_isMeasurement` is moved without any change\n+to its statement or proof. Verdict: exact preservation of both existing\n+statements, with their previously documented finite-dimensional encoding and\n+additive-error correction.\n+\n+## Stronger Auxiliary Statements\n+\n+The extracted `exists_pasting_error_of_marginal_consistency` has the original\n+homogeneous conclusion and all hypotheses except the third comparison. This\n+is justified because the existing Schmidt-mirror argument does not use that\n+comparison. The mathematical proof is retained once, rather than duplicated.\n+\n+The new `exists_pasting_error_heterogeneous` also assumes only the two forward\n+marginal comparisons. Alice and Bob have independent finite carriers. The\n+distribution, evaluation maps, projectivity conditions, conditional collision\n+bound, normalized state, nonnegative errors, universal error quantifier, and\n+pasted conclusion agree with the extracted homogeneous statement. There is\n+no dimension equality, carrier equivalence, register-exchange assumption, or\n+unproved intermediate hypothesis. Verdict: stronger auxiliary theorem, with\n+weaker assumptions and an unchanged conclusion; it is recorded in its own\n+blueprint entry, not as a replacement for the source-labelled theorem.\n+\n+The literal third homogeneous comparison cannot be placed on independent\n+carriers using a single Alice measurement. No artificial Bob measurement is\n+introduced. The original paper-facing statement continues to display that\n+comparison in its well-typed homogeneous setting.\n+\n+## Common-Space Realization\n+\n+Let the original spaces be `HA` and `HB`. Choose unit coordinate vectors\n+`a0` and `b0` and use the common space `K = HA tensor HB` on each side.\n+Alice's operators become `A tensor I`, and Bob's become `I tensor B`.\n+The new bipartite state has coefficients\n+`Psi((i,b),(a,j)) = [b=b0] [a=a0] psi(i,j)`.\n+\n+Direct finite-sum calculations prove equality of norms and of all bipartite\n+quadratic forms. Consequently the input consistency defects and the pasted\n+output defect are equal before and after this realization. Existing tensor\n+placement theorems preserve POVMs, projectivity, and postprocessing; tensor\n+multiplicativity preserves the sandwich. The distribution and collision\n+condition are untouched. A unit state rules out an empty product carrier,\n+so local nonemptiness is derived internally. No outcome nonemptiness is used.\n+\n+These constructions are closed Lean proofs and introduce no proof holes or\n+additional axioms. They do not assert an isomorphism between the original\n+matrix algebras of unequal dimensions.\ndiff --git a/blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex b/blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex\nindex 91cbea81..0cb1314e 100644\n--- a/blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex\n+++ b/blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex\n@@ -1054,6 +1054,94 @@ postprocessing, and operator-contraction steps used to formalize\n \tin \\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}, not the refuted product contract.\n \\end{proof}\n \n+\\begin{lemma}[Pasting from the forward comparisons]\n+\t\\label{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error_of_marginal_consistency}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n+\tThis formalization-only strengthening of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting} has the\n+\tsame conclusion under its projectivity and conditional collision hypotheses\n+\tand the two forward comparisons~\\eqref{eq:pasting-1}, for a unit state and\n+\t$\\eta,\\delta\\geq0$. The self-consistency comparison~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2}\n+\tis not required. The local carrier is common to both players.\n+\\end{lemma}\n+\\begin{proof}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{lem:pasting-one-sided-explicit-error, lem:pasting-coarse-commutator,\n+\t\tlem:pasting-measurement, lem:pasting-scalar-error, lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n+\tThe Schmidt-mirror proof uses only the two forward marginal comparisons,\n+\tso its error estimate proves this stronger assertion directly.\n+\\end{proof}\n+\n+\\begin{definition}[Adjoining fixed basis vectors]\n+\t\\label{def:pasting-product-state}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.productPaddedState}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\tLet $\\mathcal H_A=\\mathbb C^{I_A}$ and $\\mathcal H_B=\\mathbb C^{I_B}$,\n+\twhere $I_A,I_B$ are finite, and fix $a_0\\in I_A$, $b_0\\in I_B$.\n+\tFor $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$, define a vector on\n+\t$(\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B)^{\\otimes2}$ by\n+\t\\[\n+\t\t\\Psi_{(i,b),(a,j)}\n+\t\t=\\mathbf 1_{b=b_0}\\mathbf 1_{a=a_0}\\psi_{i,j}.\n+\t\\]\n+\tThis is a formalization-only common-space realization for\n+\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n+\\end{definition}\n+\n+\\begin{lemma}[Preservation under adjoining basis vectors]\n+\t\\label{lem:pasting-product-state-preservation}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.productPaddedState_norm}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.stateQForm_productPaddedState}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.consistencyDefect_productPaddedState}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{def:pasting-product-state, def:consistency}\n+\tFor the preceding construction, $\\|\\Psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|$. For arbitrary\n+\toperators $A$ on $\\mathcal H_A$ and $B$ on $\\mathcal H_B$,\n+\t\\[\n+\t\t\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\Psi,\n+\t\t ((A\\otimes\\Id)\\otimes(\\Id\\otimes B))\\Psi\\rangle\n+\t\t=\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\psi,(A\\otimes B)\\psi\\rangle.\n+\t\\]\n+\tConsequently every averaged off-diagonal consistency defect is preserved\n+\tby these local placements, for arbitrary operator families and any finite\n+\tdistribution. Positivity and state normalization are not required.\n+\\end{lemma}\n+\\begin{proof}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{def:pasting-product-state}\n+\tExpand the norm and quadratic form in the standard bases. The fixed\n+\tcoordinates remove two sums. Sum the quadratic-form identity over unequal\n+\tanswers and average over the question distribution.\n+\\end{proof}\n+\n+\\begin{lemma}[Pasting on independent local spaces]\n+\t\\label{lem:pasting-heterogeneous}\n+\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error_heterogeneous}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n+\tThere is a universal function\n+\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta,\\delta)=\\poly(\\eta,\\delta)$ for which\n+\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons} holds with independent finite\n+\tlocal spaces $\\mathcal H_A$ and $\\mathcal H_B$. The family $A$ acts on\n+\t$\\mathcal H_A$, the families $G_1,G_2$ act on $\\mathcal H_B$, and the\n+\tunit state belongs to $\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$. All conditional\n+\tcollision, projectivity, and forward comparison hypotheses, and the\n+\tconclusion~\\eqref{eq:pasting-3}, are retained. This is a\n+\tformalization-only strengthening of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n+\\end{lemma}\n+\\begin{proof}\n+\t\\leanok\n+\t\\uses{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons, lem:pasting-product-state-preservation,\n+\t\tlem:pasting-tensor-placement}\n+\tA unit state implies that both local spaces are nonzero. Choose one basis\n+\tvector in each and adjoin them as in Definition~\\ref{def:pasting-product-state}.\n+\tThe common local space is $\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$.\n+\tTensor placement preserves projectivity, postprocessing, and the product\n+\t$G_2G_1G_2$. Both input defects and the pasted output defect are unchanged.\n+\tApply Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons} on this common space.\n+\\end{proof}\n+\n \\begin{lemma}[Universal bound for placed consistency defects]\n \t\\label{lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n \t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_placed_le_one}\ndiff --git a/local/briefs/issue-495-heterogeneous-pasting.md b/local/briefs/issue-495-heterogeneous-pasting.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..0fc4546a\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/local/briefs/issue-495-heterogeneous-pasting.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@\n+# Issue 495: Pasting on Independent Local Spaces\n+\n+Paper sources: `lem:pasting` in `06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:504-525`,\n+and `lem:qld-xz-lines` in `14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:882-963`.\n+\n+The line-pasting application keeps Alice's and Bob's carriers distinct.\n+Realize both in their tensor product by adjoining fixed unit basis vectors.\n+Reuse the existing measurement placements and prove exact state and defect\n+preservation. Apply the stronger two-forward-comparison form of the existing\n+Schmidt-mirror argument, extracted once from its present proof.\n+\n+Preserve the public three-comparison statement `exists_pasting_error` as a\n+corollary. Record the two stronger auxiliary statements separately in the\n+blueprint. The source-labelled theorem and its certification remain unchanged.\n+The statement audit is `audits/2026-09-09-qpbt-heterogeneous-pasting.md`.\n+\n+The implementation owns only the new pasting leaf, the minimal changes to\n+`Sandwich.lean`, and corresponding documentation. It does not modify the\n+line-game transport or other active repair files.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
61229:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '368,420p' docs/CONTRIBUTING.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 5. Review Checklist\n\nEvery PR touching Lean code should be reviewed against these criteria:\n\n1. **Proof correctness** -- No unexplained `sorry`. No `axiom` unless discussed.\n   Run `rg -n \"sorry|axiom\" <file>` to verify.\n\n2. **Mathlib style** -- Follow the lean-conventions `MATHLIB_naming` and\n   `MATHLIB_doc` references. See the lean-conventions `MATHLIB_pr-review`\n   reference for the full Mathlib review guide.\n\n3. **Paper terminology** -- Public Lean names, module docstrings, declaration\n   docstrings, and documentation-visible comments should use terminology from\n   the paper and blueprint, not implementation history. See\n   [mathematical_language.md](mathematical_language.md) for the project-local\n   language rule for Lean documentation and public names. Reviewers should flag\n   names that encode historical formalization status rather than mathematical\n   content. When an old public identifier cannot be renamed in the current PR,\n   record the required migration in the issue, PR description, or an audit file\n   under [audits/](../audits/).\n   New and substantively updated audit files should follow the term norm and\n   format in\n   [audits/2026-04-29_audit-document-format.md](../audits/2026-04-29_audit-document-format.md).\n   Do not add an empty pass-through abbreviation merely to introduce a second\n   public name.\n   Review-fix PRs must read the relevant audit files under [audits/](../audits/)\n   before changing names or prose. If an audit marks a naming migration or\n   historical-formalization term as in scope for the fix, addressing it or\n   explicitly updating the audit trail is a merge blocker.\n\n4. **Linter hygiene** -- Linter-warning cleanup must fix warnings, not mask\n   them with broad `set_option linter.<name> false` blocks. See\n   [project_conventions.md](project_conventions.md#linter-warnings) for the project rule and the narrow\n   exception policy.\n\n5. **Type safety** -- No universe mismatches, coercion problems, or unresolved\n   metavariables.\n\n6. **Performance** -- Avoid expensive tactics on large types (e.g., `decide` on\n   `Fin 1000`). Watch for timeout-prone proof terms.\n\n7. **Modularity** -- Are new lemmas general enough to be reused? Could any be\n   upstreamed to Mathlib?\n\n8. **Documentation** -- Every new `def` and major `theorem` must have a docstring.\n   Module files should have a header comment with `## References` citing the\n   relevant arXiv paper(s).\n\n9. **Blueprint sync and paper origin** -- If the PR formalizes a statement\n   from the blueprint, add `\\lean{LeanDeclName}` and `\\leanok` tags to the\n   corresponding `blueprint/src/chapter/*.tex` file. If the PR adds a public\n   auxiliary lemma that is not a named statement in the original paper, record\n   that explicitly: the Lean docstring should call it a formalization-only\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
61239:+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\chapter{Games, strategies, and conditionally linear distributions}\n     2\t\\label{chap:qpbt-games}\n     3\t% Paper label sec:games kept for cross-reference fidelity; resolves to the chapter number.\n     4\t\\label{sec:games}\n     5\t\n     6\tThis chapter records the vocabulary in which the quantum Pauli basis test and its analysis are phrased: two-player one-round games and tensor product strategies, the consistency and state-dependent distance calculus for families of measurements, and conditionally linear functions and distributions, the class of question distributions used by the tests of Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-test}. The presentation follows \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}; the distance calculus is imported there from \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} and \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}.\n     7\t\n     8\t\\section{Measurements and observables}\n     9\t\n    10\t\\begin{definition}[POVMs, sub-measurements, and observables]\\label{def:povm-conventions}\n    11\t\t\\uses{def:submeasurement}\n    12\t\t% Finiteness of the outcome set is stated explicitly here; the source leaves it implicit,\n    13\t\t% and the measurement and submeasurement definitions referenced below assume finite\n    14\t\t% outcome sets.\n    15\t\tQuantum measurements are modeled as positive operator-valued measures (POVMs). A \\emph{POVM} on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$ is a family of positive semidefinite operators $\\{M_a\\}_{a \\in S}$ indexed by outcomes $a$ ranging over a finite set $S$ such that $\\sum_a M_a = \\Id$; if the latter condition is relaxed to $\\sum_a M_a \\le \\Id$, then $\\{M_a\\}$ is a \\emph{sub-measurement}. (POVMs and sub-measurements are thus exactly the measurements and submeasurements of Definition~\\ref{def:submeasurement}.) The probability that the measurement returns outcome $a$ on a state $\\ket{\\psi}$ is\n    16\t\t\\[\n    17\t\t\t\\Prb(a) = \\bra{\\psi} M_a \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n    18\t\t\\]\n    19\t\tA POVM $M = \\{M_a\\}$ is \\emph{projective} if each operator $M_a$ is a projector ($M_a^2 = M_a$); this automatically implies that $M_a M_b = M_b M_a = 0$ for distinct outcomes $a \\ne b$. An \\emph{observable} is a unitary matrix. A \\emph{binary observable} is an observable $O$ such that $O^2 = \\Id$, i.e.\\ $O$ has eigenvalues in $\\{-1,1\\}$.\n    20\t\n    21\t\tSubscripts of a measurement index the outcome, and superscripts index different measurements. When the outcome of a measurement $\\{M^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ consists of two parts $a$ and $b$, we write $\\{M^x_a\\}$ and $\\{M^x_b\\}$ for its \\emph{marginals}\n    22\t\t\\[\n    23\t\t\tM^x_a = \\sum_b M^x_{a,\\,b}\\,, \\qquad M^x_b = \\sum_a M^x_{a,\\,b}\\,;\n    24\t\t\\]\n    25\t\tfor every $x$ both marginals are again POVMs.\n    26\t\\end{definition}\n    27\t\n    28\t\\begin{definition}[Bracket notation for post-processed measurements]\\label{def:bracket}\n    29\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.Quantum.Measurement.postprocess}\n    30\t\t\\leanok\n    31\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:post-processing}\n    32\t\tLet $\\mathcal A$ and $\\mathcal B$ be finite sets, let $\\{M^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a family of POVMs indexed by $x \\in \\mathcal X$, and let $f \\colon \\mathcal A \\to \\mathcal B$ be an arbitrary function. We write $\\bigl\\{ M^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]} \\bigr\\}$ for the POVM derived from $\\{M^x_a\\}$ by applying the function $f$ to the outcome before returning it:\n    33\t\t\\[\n    34\t\t\tM^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]} = \\sum_{a\\,:\\,f(a)=b} M^x_a\\,,\n    35\t\t\\]\n    36\t\tand $M^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]} = 0$ when $b$ is not in the image of $f$. When the outcome $a$ has a natural interpretation as a tuple $(a_1,\\dots,a_k)$, we also write, for $i \\in \\{1,\\dots,k\\}$,\n    37\t\t\\[\n    38\t\t\tM^x_{[a_i=b]} = \\sum_{(a_1,\\dots,a_k)\\,:\\,a_i=b} M^x_{(a_1,\\dots,a_k)}\\,.\n    39\t\t\\]\n    40\t\tFor each fixed $x$ this is the post-processing operation of Definition~\\ref{def:post-processing}.\n    41\t\\end{definition}\n    42\t\n    43\t\\begin{remark}[Naimark dilation for strategies]\\label{rem:naimark-for-games}\n    44\t\tThe symmetrization argument of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} below invokes Naimark dilation in the form of Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark} (stated and proved in Chapter~\\ref{chap:projective}): a bipartite state together with two sub-measurement families can be replaced, without changing any of the correlations $\\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}$, by a product-extended state together with projective sub-measurements on the enlarged spaces. When the input families are complete measurements, the dilated projective sub-measurements can be completed by assigning the residual projector of each family to one fixed outcome; the resulting projective measurements reproduce the same correlations on the extended state, since the correlation-preservation identity forces the extended state to lie in the range of the total projector of each dilated family. This completed form, with genuinely projective measurements, is the one used in Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}; for the proof the source refers to Theorem 5.1 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}, which is stated for complete measurements. The source follows its statement of the dilation theorem with a sentence announcing an ``orthogonalization lemma'' from the literature that is never actually stated there; nothing in this chapter depends on it.\n    45\t\\end{remark}\n    46\t\n    47\t\\section{Games and strategies}\n    48\t% Paper subsection label sec:strat kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n    49\t\\label{sec:strat}\n    50\t\n    51\t\\begin{definition}[Two-player one-round games]\\label{def:game}\n    52\t\tA \\emph{two-player one-round game} $\\mathfrak G$ is specified by a tuple $(\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ where\n    53\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n    54\t\t\t\\item $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal Y$ are finite sets, called the \\emph{question alphabets},\n    55\t\t\t\\item $\\mathcal A$ and $\\mathcal B$ are finite sets, called the \\emph{answer alphabets},\n    56\t\t\t\\item $\\mu$ is a probability distribution over $\\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y$, called the \\emph{question distribution}, and\n    57\t\t\t\\item $D \\colon \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y \\times \\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal B \\to \\{0,1\\}$ is a function, called the \\emph{decision predicate}.\n    58\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n    59\t\\end{definition}\n    60\t\n    61\t\\begin{definition}[Tensor product strategies]\\label{def:tensor-product-strategy}\n    62\t\t\\uses{def:game, def:povm-conventions}\n    63\t\tA \\emph{tensor product strategy} $\\mathcal S$ for a game $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ is a tuple $(\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ where\n    64\t\t\\begin{itemize}\n    65\t\t\t\\item $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a pure quantum state, i.e.\\ a unit vector in $\\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ for finite-dimensional complex Hilbert spaces $\\mathcal H_A$, $\\mathcal H_B$,\n    66\t\t\t\\item $A$ is a set $\\{A^x\\}$ such that for every $x \\in \\mathcal X$, $A^x = \\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is a POVM on $\\mathcal H_A$, and\n    67\t\t\t\\item $B$ is a set $\\{B^y\\}$ such that for every $y \\in \\mathcal Y$, $B^y = \\{B^y_b\\}_{b \\in \\mathcal B}$ is a POVM on $\\mathcal H_B$.\n    68\t\t\\end{itemize}\n    69\t\\end{definition}\n    70\t\n    71\t\\begin{definition}[Tensor product value]\\label{def:tensor-product-value}\n    72\t\t\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy}\n    73\t\tThe \\emph{tensor product value} of a tensor product strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ with respect to a game $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ is\n    74\t\t\\[\n    75\t\t\t\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) = \\sum_{x,\\,y,\\,a,\\,b} \\mu(x,y)\\, D(x,y,a,b)\\, \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n    76\t\t\\]\n    77\t\tFor $v \\in [0,1]$ we say that the strategy $\\mathcal S$ \\emph{passes (or wins) $\\mathfrak G$ with probability $v$} if $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) \\ge v$. The \\emph{tensor product value} of $\\mathfrak G$ is\n    78\t\t\\[\n    79\t\t\t\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G) = \\sup_{\\mathcal S} \\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S)\\,,\n    80\t\t\\]\n    81\t\twhere the supremum is taken over all tensor product strategies $\\mathcal S$ for $\\mathfrak G$.\n    82\t\\end{definition}\n    83\t\n    84\tUnless specified otherwise, all strategies considered in what follows are tensor product strategies, and we simply call them \\emph{strategies}; similarly, we refer to $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ as the \\emph{value} of the game $\\mathfrak G$.\n    85\t\n    86\t% The source labels this definition def:projective-strategy. That label is already carried\n    87\t% by the low individual degree test track (Chapter 2), where it names the strategy format\n    88\t% specific to that test, so a distinct label is used here; see the following remark.\n    89\t\\begin{definition}[Projective strategies]\\label{def:projective-strategy-general}\n    90\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Strategy.IsProjective}\n    91\t\t\\leanok\n    92\t\t\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy, def:povm-conventions}\n    93\t\tA strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ is \\emph{projective} if all the measurements $\\{A^x_a\\}_a$ and $\\{B^y_b\\}_b$ are projective.\n    94\t\\end{definition}\n    95\t\n    96\t\\begin{remark}[Two uses of the term ``projective strategy'']\\label{rem:projective-strategy-relation}\n    97\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:projective-strategy-general} is the general notion: a strategy for an arbitrary nonlocal game all of whose measurements are projective. The low individual degree chapters use the same term for the concrete data of a two-prover strategy for the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test (Definition~\\ref{def:projective-strategy} in Chapter~\\ref{chap:test}); that definition is the specialization of the present notion to the question format of that particular test. The two usages occur in disjoint contexts.\n    98\t\\end{remark}\n    99\t\n   100\t\\begin{definition}[Symmetric games and symmetric strategies]\\label{def:symmetric-game}\n   101\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricGame, MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricGame.toGame, MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricStrategy, MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricStrategy.toStrategy}\n   102\t\t\\leanok\n   103\t\t% Paper label: rem:symmetric-games (a definition environment carrying a rem: label in the\n   104\t\t% source); relabeled here to match the environment kind.\n   105\t\t\\uses{def:game, def:tensor-product-strategy}\n   106\t\tA game $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ is \\emph{symmetric} if the question and answer alphabets are the same for both players (i.e.\\ $\\mathcal X = \\mathcal Y$ and $\\mathcal A = \\mathcal B$), the distribution $\\mu$ is symmetric (i.e.\\ $\\mu(x,y) = \\mu(y,x)$ for all $x,y$), and the decision predicate $D$ treats both players symmetrically (i.e.\\ $D(x,y,a,b) = D(y,x,b,a)$ for all $x,y,a,b$).\n   107\t\n   108\t\tA strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ is \\emph{symmetric} if $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a (pure) state in $\\mathcal H \\ot \\mathcal H$, for some Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$, that is invariant under permutation of the two factors, and the measurement operators of both players are identical.\n   109\t\\end{definition}\n   110\t\n   111\tWe often specify symmetric games $\\mathfrak G$ and symmetric strategies $\\mathcal S$ using a compact notation: we write $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A, \\mu, D)$ and $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$, where $M$ denotes the set of measurement operators shared by both players.\n   112\t\n   113\t% Source statement (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:94-99) kept in\n   114\t% its original form. The proof below records the approximate form the source argument\n   115\t% establishes and leaves the final attainment step unproved; see rem:symmetric-strat-limit.\n   116\t\\begin{lemma}[Symmetrization of strategies for symmetric games]\\label{lem:symmetric-strat}\n   117\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_symmetric_projective_strategy}\n   118\t\t\\uses{def:symmetric-game, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   119\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A, \\mu, D)$ be a symmetric game such that $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G) = 1-\\eps$ for some $\\eps \\ge 0$. Then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$ such that $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) \\ge 1-\\eps$.\n   120\t\\end{lemma}\n   121\t\\begin{proof}\n   122\t\t\\uses{thm:naimark}\n   123\t\tThe symmetrization argument establishes the following approximate form of the conclusion: for every $\\eps' > \\eps$ there exists a symmetric and projective strategy of value at least $1-\\eps'$. Indeed, fix $\\eps' > \\eps$. Since $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ is the supremum of the values of all strategies, there exists a strategy $\\mathcal S' = (\\ket{\\psi'}, A, B)$ with $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S') \\ge 1-\\eps'$. Using Naimark dilation (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}), each dilated sub-measurement being completed to a projective measurement by assigning to one fixed outcome the residual projector of its family --- which changes no correlation on the extended state, as described in Remark~\\ref{rem:naimark-for-games} above --- we may assume without loss of generality that $\\ket{\\psi'} \\in \\C^d_{A'} \\ot \\C^d_{B'}$ for some integer $d$ and that every $A^x$ and $B^x$ is a projective measurement. Let\n   124\t\t\\[\n   125\t\t\t\\ket{\\psi} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\, \\bigl( \\ket{0}_{A}\\ket{1}_{B}\\ket{\\psi'}_{A'B'} + \\ket{1}_{A}\\ket{0}_{B}\\ket{\\psi'_\\tau}_{A'B'} \\bigr) \\in \\bigl(\\C^2_{A} \\ot \\C^d_{A'}\\bigr) \\ot \\bigl(\\C^2_{B} \\ot \\C^d_{B'}\\bigr)\\,,\n   126\t\t\\]\n   127\t\twhere $\\ket{\\psi'_\\tau}$ is obtained from $\\ket{\\psi'}$ by permuting the two players' registers; then $\\ket{\\psi}$ is invariant under permutation of the register pairs $AA'$ and $BB'$. For each question $x \\in \\mathcal X$ define the projective measurement $M^x = \\{M^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ on $\\C^2 \\ot \\C^d$ by\n   128\t\t\\[\n   129\t\t\tM^x_a = \\ket{0}\\bra{0} \\ot A^x_a + \\ket{1}\\bra{1} \\ot B^x_a\\,,\n   130\t\t\\]\n   131\t\tand let each player measure $M$ on their pair of registers. Because the decision predicate $D$ of the symmetric game treats the two players symmetrically, the resulting symmetric projective strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$ satisfies $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) = \\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S') \\ge 1-\\eps'$. Note that this construction preserves the value of the strategy it is applied to exactly.\n   132\t\n   133\t\tThe statement asserts more, namely a single symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\\eps$ itself. Passing from the family of strategies constructed above to one attaining the bound $1-\\eps$ is an attainment step which the argument does not supply: the supremum defining $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:tensor-product-value} ranges over strategies of unbounded dimension and need not be attained. This step remains unproved; the obstruction, and the forms of the lemma that the argument does prove, are documented in \\cite{gap:qpbt_symmetrization-attainment}. See also Remark~\\ref{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}.\n   134\t\\end{proof}\n   135\t\n   136\t\\begin{remark}[Attainment in the symmetrization lemma]\\label{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}\n   137\t\tThe proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} yields, for each $\\eps' > \\eps$, a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\\eps'$. The conclusion as stated, at $\\eps' = \\eps$, does not follow from this: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\\eps' \\downarrow \\eps$, and the supremum defining $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. The symmetrization construction itself is value-preserving, so the conclusion does hold whenever the hypothesis supplies an actual strategy of value at least $1-\\eps$ rather than only the value of the game; every use of the lemma in the source proceeds from such a strategy, or from a strict lower bound on the value, and therefore consumes only what the proof establishes. The gap and the available repairs are documented in \\cite{gap:qpbt_symmetrization-attainment}.\n   138\t\\end{remark}\n   139\t\n   140\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}]\n   141\t\t\\label{lem:symmetric-strat-given-strategy}\n   142\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_symmetric_projective_strategy_of_strategy}\n   143\t\t\\leanok\n   144\t\t\\uses{def:symmetric-game, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   145\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it is the value-preserving given-strategy form of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} isolated in Remark~\\ref{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}, and it is what the symmetrization argument actually proves. Let $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A, \\mu, D)$ be a symmetric game and let $\\mathcal S'$ be a strategy for $\\mathfrak G$ with $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S') \\ge 1-\\eps$. Then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$ with\n   146\t\t\\[\n   147\t\t\t\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) = \\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S') \\ge 1-\\eps\\,.\n   148\t\t\\]\n   149\t\\end{lemma}\n   150\t\\begin{proof}\n   151\t\t\\leanok\n   152\t\t\\uses{thm:naimark}\n   153\t\tApply Naimark dilation (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}) to $\\mathcal S'$, completing each dilated family at one fixed outcome as described in Remark~\\ref{rem:naimark-for-games}, and then form the permutation-invariant state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and the block-diagonal measurements $M^x$ of the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:symmetric-strat}. Both steps preserve the value of the strategy they are applied to, so $\\mathcal S$ has exactly the value of $\\mathcal S'$. No attainment step is involved, since the strategy $\\mathcal S'$ is given rather than extracted from the supremum defining $\\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G)$.\n   154\t\\end{proof}\n   155\t\n   156\t\\begin{definition}[Commuting strategies]\\label{def:comm-strategy}\n   157\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.IsCommutingOn, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Strategy.IsCommuting}\n   158\t\t\\leanok\n   159\t\t\\uses{def:game, def:tensor-product-strategy}\n   160\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ be a game, and let $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ be a strategy for $\\mathfrak G$ such that $\\mathcal H_A = \\mathcal H_B$. We say that $\\mathcal S$ is a \\emph{commuting strategy for $\\mathfrak G$} if for all question pairs $(x,y)$ in the support of $\\mu$ (i.e.\\ all $(x,y)$ with $\\mu(x,y) > 0$) we have\n   161\t\t\\[\n   162\t\t\t[A^x_a, B^y_b] = 0 \\qquad \\text{for all } a \\in \\mathcal A,\\ b \\in \\mathcal B\\,,\n   163\t\t\\]\n   164\t\twhere $[A,B] = AB - BA$ denotes the commutator.\n   165\t\\end{definition}\n   166\t\n   167\t\\begin{definition}[Consistent measurements]\\label{def:consistent-measurement}\n   168\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.IsConsistentOn}\n   169\t\t\\leanok\n   170\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   171\t\tLet $\\mathcal A$ be a finite set, let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H \\ot \\mathcal H$ be a state, and let $\\{M_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a projective measurement on $\\mathcal H$. We say that \\emph{$\\{M_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$} if and only if\n   172\t\t\\[\n   173\t\t\t\\forall a \\in \\mathcal A\\,, \\qquad M_a \\ot \\Id_B\\, \\ket{\\psi} = \\Id_A \\ot M_a\\, \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n   174\t\t\\]\n   175\t\tWhen the state $\\ket{\\psi}$ is clear from context we simply say that $\\{M_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is consistent.\n   176\t\\end{definition}\n   177\t\n   178\tThe terminology arises from the fact that when the measurement $\\{M_a\\}$ is performed on both registers of $\\ket{\\psi}$, the probability of obtaining twice the same outcome is\n   179\t\\[\n   180\t\t\\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} M_a \\ot M_a \\ket{\\psi} = \\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} \\Id_A \\ot (M_a)^2 \\ket{\\psi} = \\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} \\Id_A \\ot M_a \\ket{\\psi} = 1\\,,\n   181\t\\]\n   182\twhere the first equality is by the definition of consistency, the second uses that $\\{M_a\\}$ is projective, and the last uses $\\sum_a M_a = \\Id$.\n   183\t\n   184\t\\begin{definition}[Consistent strategies]\\label{def:consistent-strategy}\n   185\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.IsConsistentStrategyOn, MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricStrategy.IsConsistent}\n   186\t\t\\leanok\n   187\t\t\\uses{def:consistent-measurement, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   188\t\tLet $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ be a projective strategy with state $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H \\ot \\mathcal H$, for some Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$, defined on question alphabets $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal Y$ and answer alphabets $\\mathcal A$ and $\\mathcal B$, respectively. We say that $\\mathcal S$ is \\emph{consistent} if for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ the measurement $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$, and for all $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ the measurement $\\{B^y_b\\}_{b \\in \\mathcal B}$ is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$.\n   189\t\\end{definition}\n   190\t\n   191\tWe almost always restrict attention to symmetric strategies; in this case consistency implies that whenever both players are sent the same question, they provide the same answer.\n   192\t\n   193\t\\begin{definition}[PCC and SPCC strategies]\\label{def:spcc}\n   194\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Strategy.IsPCC, MIPStarRE.QPBT.SymmetricStrategy.IsSPCC}\n   195\t\t\\leanok\n   196\t\t\\uses{def:projective-strategy-general, def:consistent-strategy, def:comm-strategy, def:symmetric-game}\n   197\t\tWe say that a strategy $\\mathcal S$ for a game $\\mathfrak G$ is \\emph{PCC} if it is projective, consistent, and commuting for $\\mathfrak G$. Additionally, we say that a PCC strategy $\\mathcal S$ is \\emph{SPCC} if it is furthermore symmetric.\n   198\t\\end{definition}\n   199\t\n   200\t\\section{Distance measures}\n   201\t\n   202\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between states]\\label{def:state-distance}\n   203\t\tLet $\\{\\ket{\\psi_n}\\}_{n \\in \\N}$ and $\\{\\ket{\\psi'_n}\\}_{n \\in \\N}$ be two families of states in the same space $\\mathcal H$. For a function $\\delta \\colon \\N \\to [0,1]$ we say that $\\{\\ket{\\psi_n}\\}$ and $\\{\\ket{\\psi'_n}\\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-close}, denoted $\\ket{\\psi} \\approx_\\delta \\ket{\\psi'}$, if\n   204\t\t\\[\n   205\t\t\t\\lVert \\ket{\\psi_n} - \\ket{\\psi'_n} \\rVert^2 = O(\\delta(n))\\,.\n   206\t\t\\]\n   207\t\tThe dependence of the states and of $\\delta$ on the indexing parameter $n$ is generally left implicit; we write, e.g., $\\ket{\\psi}$ for $\\ket{\\psi_n}$.\n   208\t\\end{definition}\n   209\t\n   210\t\\begin{definition}[Consistency between POVMs]\\label{def:consistency}\n   211\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect, MIPStarRE.QPBT.IsConsistentWithin}\n   212\t\t\\leanok\n   213\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\n   214\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs. We write\n   215\t\t\\[\n   216\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a\n   217\t\t\\]\n   218\t\ton state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and distribution $\\mu$ if\n   219\t\t\\[\n   220\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi} = O(\\delta)\\,.\n   221\t\t\\]\n   222\t\tIn this case we say that $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-consistent} on $\\ket{\\psi}$. As in Definition~\\ref{def:state-distance}, the $O(\\cdot)$ refers to families of POVMs and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and is taken as $n \\to \\infty$.\n   223\t\\end{definition}\n   224\t\n   225\tA measurement that is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$ in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same state under the singleton distribution, and vice versa: if $\\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} M_a \\ot M_a \\ket{\\psi} = 1$, then $M_a \\ot \\Id\\, \\ket{\\psi} = \\Id \\ot M_a\\, \\ket{\\psi}$ for every $a$, by the equality case of the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality applied outcome by outcome.\n   226\t\n   227\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:257-274) states this\n   228\t% definition for pairs of POVMs but applies the relation to arbitrary operator families:\n   229\t% to plain operators at 06:313-330, to sets of matrices at 06:364-380, and to products and\n   230\t% commutators, compared against the zero family, at 06:409-445. The general form below,\n   231\t% for arbitrary finite families of operators, is the one of the NEEXP source\n   232\t% (references/neexp-paper/05_quantum_preliminaries.tex:385-391), from which the distance\n   233\t% calculus is imported; the POVM case is singled out after the display.\n   234\t\\begin{definition}[State-dependent distance between operator families]\\label{def:povm-distance}\n   235\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\n   236\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal A$ be finite sets and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{M^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ and $\\{N^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be two families of linear operators on $\\mathcal H$. We say that \\emph{$\\{M^x_a\\}$ and $\\{N^x_a\\}$ are $\\delta$-close on state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and under distribution $\\mu$} if\n   237\t\t\\[\n   238\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\sum_a \\lVert (M^x_a - N^x_a)\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le O(\\delta)\\,,\n   239\t\t\\]\n   240\t\tand we write $M^x_a \\approx_\\delta N^x_a$ to denote this when the state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and the distribution $\\mu$ are clear from context. This distance is referred to as the \\emph{state-dependent} distance. The principal case is that in which $\\{M^x_a\\}$ and $\\{N^x_a\\}$ are POVMs on $\\mathcal H$, and we then speak of the state-dependent distance between the POVMs; but the relation is used for arbitrary operator families --- for instance for products of measurement operators, or for commutators compared against the identically zero family, as in Lemma~\\ref{lem:commutation-analysis} below.\n   241\t\\end{definition}\n   242\t\n   243\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between strategies]\\label{def:strategy-distance}\n   244\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.AreCloseStrategies}\n   245\t\t\\leanok\n   246\t\t\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy, def:state-distance, def:povm-distance}\n   247\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ be a nonlocal game and let $\\mathcal S = (\\psi, A, B)$ and $\\mathcal S' = (\\psi', A', B')$ be strategies for $\\mathfrak G$. For $\\delta \\in [0,1]$ we say that \\emph{$\\mathcal S$ is $\\delta$-close to $\\mathcal S'$} if the following conditions hold.\n   248\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   249\t\t\t\\item The states $\\ket{\\psi}, \\ket{\\psi'}$ are states in the same Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ and are $\\delta$-close.\n   250\t\t\t\\item For all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$, we have $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta (A')^x_a$ and $B^y_b \\approx_\\delta (B')^y_b$, with the approximations holding under the distribution $\\mu$ (i.e.\\ under its corresponding marginals), on either $\\ket{\\psi}$ or $\\ket{\\psi'}$.\n   251\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   252\t\\end{definition}\n   253\t\n   254\t\\begin{remark}[Asymptotic reading of the distance relations]\\label{rem:asymptotic-distance}\n   255\t\tDefinitions~\\ref{def:state-distance}, \\ref{def:consistency}, and \\ref{def:povm-distance} are asymptotic: every $\\approx_\\delta$ and $\\simeq_\\delta$ statement refers to families of states and measurement families indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and the $O(\\cdot)$ is taken as $n \\to \\infty$. None of these relations is a pointwise inequality with an explicit constant. By contrast, the corresponding relations used in the low individual degree chapters (Definitions~\\ref{def:simeq} and~\\ref{def:approx_delta}) carry no hidden constant. Any explicit quantitative version of the present relations therefore retains the constants absorbed into the $O(\\cdot)$; for instance, the triangle inequality of Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} carries a factor $2$ inside the $O(\\cdot)$.\n   256\t\\end{remark}\n   257\t\n   258\tWe record several useful facts about the consistency measure and the state-dependent distance. For the statements imported without proof, readers are referred to Sections~4.4 and~4.5 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   259\t\n   260\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency versus closeness; Fact 4.13 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:agreement}\n   261\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_le_two_mul_consistencyDefect, MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_le_opFamilyDistSq_of_projective}\n   262\t\t\\leanok\n   263\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   264\t\tFor POVMs $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$, the following hold.\n   265\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   266\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   267\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ \\emph{and} $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are projective measurements, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   268\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   269\t\\end{lemma}\n   270\t\\begin{proof}\n   271\t\t\\leanok\n   272\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-consistency-term,\n   273\t\t\tlem:distance-point-distance-le-defect,\n   274\t\t\tlem:distance-projective-point-distance, lem:distance-nonnegative}\n   275\t\tStated in the source without proof; this is Fact 4.13 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \\le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \\le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ bounds the total by at most twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\\delta)$ by hypothesis. When both families are projective the two operator inequalities hold with equality, so the expansion equals exactly twice the off-diagonal mass; reading this identity in the other direction gives the second item.\n   276\t\\end{proof}\n   277\t\n   278\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency from closeness with one projective family; Fact 4.14 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:agreement-one-sided}\n   279\t\t% Status: the statement is final; no Lean declaration exists yet for the full disjunctive form, so this node carries no formalization marker.\n   280\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   281\t\tLet $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs. If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and either $\\{A^x_a\\}$ or $\\{B^x_a\\}$ is a projective measurement, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\delta^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   282\t\\end{lemma}\n   283\t\\begin{proof}\n   284\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root}\n   285\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.14 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Write the off-diagonal mass $\\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}$ as an inner product involving $(A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi}$, using projectivity of one of the two families, and apply the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality; this route costs a square root in the error. Only the branch in which $\\{A^x_a\\}$ is the projective family is available, as Lemma~\\ref{lem:agreement-projective-left}; the branch in which only $\\{B^x_a\\}$ is projective is still open.\n   286\t\\end{proof}\n   287\t\n   288\t\\begin{lemma}[Left-projective branch of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement-one-sided}]\\label{lem:agreement-projective-left}\n   289\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_le_sqrt_of_projective_left}\n   290\t\t\\leanok\n   291\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   292\t\tThis entry states only the branch of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement-one-sided} in which the left family is the projective one; it is not the full disjunctive statement of the source. Let $\\mu$ be a probability distribution on $\\mathcal X$, let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a unit vector, and let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ be a projective measurement for every $x$. Then\n   293\t\t\\[\n   294\t\t\t\\E_x \\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}\n   295\t\t\t\\leq \\sqrt{2\\, \\E_x \\sum_a \\bigl\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2}\\,;\n   296\t\t\\]\n   297\t\tin particular, if $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{(2\\delta)^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   298\t\\end{lemma}\n   299\t\\begin{proof}\n   300\t\t\\leanok\n   301\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root, lem:distance-averaged-square-root}\n   302\t\tApply the pointwise estimate of Lemma~\\ref{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root} at each question $x$, then average over $x$ with Lemma~\\ref{lem:distance-averaged-square-root}.\n   303\t\\end{proof}\n   304\t\n   305\t\\begin{lemma}[Fact 4.20 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:add-a-proj}\n   306\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_mul_left_le}\n   307\t\t\\leanok\n   308\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   309\t\tLet $\\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mathcal C$ be finite sets, and let $\\mu$ be a distribution over question pairs $(x,y)$. Let $\\{A^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ and $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ be operators whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal B$. Suppose a set of operators $\\{C^y_{a,\\,c}\\}$, whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal C$, satisfies the condition $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$ for all $a$ and all $y$. If $A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $x$ sampled from the corresponding marginal of the distribution $\\mu$, then $C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $(x,y)$ sampled from $\\mu$.\n   310\t\\end{lemma}\n   311\t\\begin{proof}\n   312\t\t\\leanok\n   313\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-sum-contraction}\n   314\t\tFix questions $x, y$ and answers $a \\in \\mathcal A$, $b \\in \\mathcal B$. Then\n   315\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   316\t\t\t\\sum_c \\bigl\\lVert (C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} - C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   317\t\t\t&= \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\Bigr) (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b}) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   318\t\t\t&\\le \\bigl\\lVert (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\\,,\n   319\t\t\\end{align*}\n   320\t\twhere the inequality uses the assumption $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$. Summing over $a, b$ and averaging over $(x,y) \\sim \\mu$ yields the conclusion.\n   321\t\\end{proof}\n   322\t\n   323\t\\begin{lemma}[Multiplying by function-indexed operators]\\label{fact:add-a-proj2}\n   324\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_mul_funIndexed_le}\n   325\t\t\\leanok\n   326\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   327\t\tLet $\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A$ be finite sets, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of functions $g \\colon \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs indexed by $\\mathcal X$ with outcomes in $\\mathcal A$. Let $\\{S^x_g\\}_{x \\in \\mathcal X,\\, g \\in \\mathcal G}$ be a set of operators such that for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$, $\\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ on average over $x$, then $S^x_g A^x_{g(x)} \\approx_\\delta S^x_g B^x_{g(x)}$.\n   328\t\\end{lemma}\n   329\t\\begin{proof}\n   330\t\t\\leanok\n   331\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-function-indexed-contraction}\n   332\t\tExpand the left-hand side and group the sum over $g$ according to the value $a = g(x)$:\n   333\t\t\\[\n   334\t\t\t\\E_x \\sum_g \\bigl\\lVert S^x_g (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   335\t\t\t= \\E_x \\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\Bigr) (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n   336\t\t\\]\n   337\t\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:347-361) ends with\n   338\t\t% \"at most delta by assumption\"; under def:povm-distance the hypothesis only bounds this\n   339\t\t% quantity by O(delta), which is what the conclusion requires.\n   340\t\tSince $\\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$, this is at most $\\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$, which is $O(\\delta)$ by the hypothesis $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}); this is exactly the bound required for the conclusion.\n   341\t\\end{proof}\n   342\t\n   343\t\\begin{lemma}[Absorbing an approximating family into a projective sum]\\label{lem:cool-closeness-fact}\n   344\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opDistSq_sum_sub_mul_le_of_projective}\n   345\t\t\\leanok\n   346\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\n   347\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal A$ be finite sets and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a projective measurement and let $\\{B^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a set of matrices. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$, then for all subsets $S \\subseteq \\mathcal A$ we have\n   348\t\t\\[\n   349\t\t\t\\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\approx_\\delta \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\cdot B^x_a\\,.\n   350\t\t\\]\n   351\t\\end{lemma}\n   352\t\\begin{proof}\n   353\t\t\\leanok\n   354\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-orthogonality,\n   355\t\t\tlem:distance-projective-sum}\n   356\t\tBy projectivity, $\\sum_{a \\in S} (A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a) = \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a)$. Expanding the squared norm of the corresponding vector, the cross terms for distinct $a \\ne a' \\in S$ vanish because $A^x_a A^x_{a'} = 0$, so\n   357\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   358\t\t\t\\E_x \\Bigl\\lVert \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bigl(A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a\\bigr) \\ket{\\psi} \\Bigr\\rVert^2\n   359\t\t\t&= \\E_x \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   360\t\t\t&\\le \\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2\\,,\n   361\t\t\\end{align*}\n   362\t\t% Under def:povm-distance the closeness hypothesis bounds the final quantity by\n   363\t\t% O(delta), not by delta itself; the source\n   364\t\t% (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:364-380) elides this.\n   365\t\twhere the last step uses $A^x_a \\le \\Id$. All expectations are taken according to $\\mu$, and the last quantity is $O(\\delta)$ by the assumption $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}), which is the bound required for the conclusion.\n   366\t\\end{proof}\n   367\t\n   368\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality; Fact 4.28 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:triangle}\n   369\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_le_of_le_of_le}\n   370\t\t\\leanok\n   371\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   372\t\tIf $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ and $B^x_a \\approx_{\\eps} C^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\approx_{\\delta+\\eps} C^x_a$.\n   373\t\\end{lemma}\n   374\t\\begin{proof}\n   375\t\t\\leanok\n   376\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   377\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.28 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For every $x$ and $a$, $\\lVert (A^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le 2\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 + 2\\lVert (B^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$. Averaging over $x$ and summing over $a$ gives the claim; the factor $2$ is absorbed by the $O(\\cdot)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\n   378\t\\end{proof}\n   379\t\n   380\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\simeq$''; Proposition 4.29 in \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}]\\label{fact:triangle-for-simeq}\n   381\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_trans_le}\n   382\t\t\\leanok\n   383\t\t\\uses{def:consistency}\n   384\t\tIf $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\gamma \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, then\n   385\t\t\\[\n   386\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps + 2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}} \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a\\,.\n   387\t\t\\]\n   388\t\\end{lemma}\n   389\t\\begin{proof}\n   390\t\t\\leanok\n   391\t\t\\uses{fact:agreement, fact:triangle, lem:distance-overlap-gap,\n   392\t\t\tlem:distance-defect-overlap, lem:distance-symmetry}\n   393\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Proposition 4.29 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The two hypotheses involving $C$ convert, via Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, into state-dependent closeness of $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B$ to both $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, hence of $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ to $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$ at cost $O(\\delta+\\gamma)$. The first consistency then transfers from $B$ to $D$ by expanding the off-diagonal mass and applying the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, which turns the squared-distance bound into the term $2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}$.\n   394\t\\end{proof}\n   395\t\n   396\t\\begin{lemma}[Data processing; Fact 4.26 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:data-processing}\n   397\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_postprocess_le}\n   398\t\t\\leanok\n   399\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket}\n   400\t\tSuppose $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$. Then for any function $f$ on the outcome set,\n   401\t\t\\[\n   402\t\t\tA^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]}\\,.\n   403\t\t\\]\n   404\t\\end{lemma}\n   405\t\\begin{proof}\n   406\t\t\\leanok\n   407\t\t\\uses{def:distance-state-quadratic-form,\n   408\t\t\tlem:distance-consistency-term, lem:distance-point-defect,\n   409\t\t\tdef:distance-left-placed-measurement,\n   410\t\t\tdef:distance-right-placed-measurement,\n   411\t\t\tlem:distance-placed-product-qform,\n   412\t\t\tlem:distance-postprocess-overlap}\n   413\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.26 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Every off-diagonal pair $(b, b')$ with $b \\ne b'$ of the post-processed families pulls back to pairs $(a, a')$ with $f(a) = b \\ne b' = f(a')$, and in particular $a \\ne a'$. Coarse-graining therefore only merges terms of the off-diagonal sum, so the post-processed off-diagonal mass is at most the original one.\n   414\t\\end{proof}\n   415\t\n   416\tThe state-dependent distance is the right tool for reasoning about the closeness of measurement operators in a strategy. The following lemma converts joint consistency with a single projective measurement into an approximate commutation relation.\n   417\t\n   418\t\\begin{lemma}[Approximate commutation from joint consistency]\\label{lem:commutation-analysis}\n   419\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opDistSq_commutator_le}\n   420\t\t\\leanok\n   421\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\n   422\t\tLet $\\{A^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$, $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\}$, $\\{C^x_{a,\\,c}\\}$ be POVMs. Suppose $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\}$ is projective, and\n   423\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   424\t\t\tA^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot \\Id_B &\\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b}\\,,\\\\\n   425\t\t\tC^x_{a,\\,c} \\ot \\Id_B &\\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c}\\,,\n   426\t\t\\end{align*}\n   427\t\twhere $B^x_{a,\\,b} = \\sum_c B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}$ and $B^x_{a,\\,c} = \\sum_b B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}$ are the marginals of Definition~\\ref{def:povm-conventions}. Then the following approximate commutation relation holds:\n   428\t\t\\[\n   429\t\t\t[A^x_{a,\\,b},\\, C^x_{a,\\,c}] \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta 0\\,.\n   430\t\t\\]\n   431\t\\end{lemma}\n   432\t\\begin{proof}\n   433\t\t\\leanok\n   434\t\t\\uses{fact:add-a-proj, fact:triangle,\n   435\t\t\tlem:distance-left-fiber-contraction,\n   436\t\t\tlem:distance-right-fiber-contraction,\n   437\t\t\tlem:distance-joint-marginal-product,\n   438\t\t\tlem:distance-joint-marginal-product-rev,\n   439\t\t\tlem:distance-opposite-placements-commute, lem:distance-reindex,\n   440\t\t\tlem:distance-congr, lem:distance-congr-sub,\n   441\t\t\tdef:distance-swap-last, lem:distance-swap-last-inverse,\n   442\t\t\tlem:distance-symmetry}\n   443\t\tApplying Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to $C^x_{a,\\,c} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c}$ with the multiplier family $\\{A^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot \\Id_B\\}$, we have\n   444\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-1}\n   445\t\t\tA^x_{a,\\,b}\\, C^x_{a,\\,c} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta A^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c}\\,.\n   446\t\t\\end{equation}\n   447\t\tSimilarly, applying Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to $A^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b}$ with the multiplier family $\\{\\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c}\\}$, and using that $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\}$ is projective,\n   448\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-2}\n   449\t\t\tA^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c} \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,c}\\, B^x_{a,\\,b} = \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\,.\n   450\t\t\\end{equation}\n   451\t\tCombining \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-1} and \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-2} via Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle},\n   452\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-3}\n   453\t\t\tA^x_{a,\\,b}\\, C^x_{a,\\,c} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\,.\n   454\t\t\\end{equation}\n   455\t\tA symmetric argument gives\n   456\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-4}\n   457\t\t\tC^x_{a,\\,c}\\, A^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\,.\n   458\t\t\\end{equation}\n   459\t\tThe claim follows by subtracting \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-3} and \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-4}.\n   460\t\\end{proof}\n   461\t\n   462\t\\begin{lemma}[Right-register commutation; formalization support]\n   463\t\t\\label{lem:commutation-analysis-right}\n   464\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opDistSq_commutator_right_le}\n   465\t\t\\leanok\n   466\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\n   467\t\tThere is a universal constant $C_0 \\ge 1$ with the following property.\n   468\t\tLet $\\mu$ be a question distribution, $\\ket{\\psi}$ a bipartite vector,\n   469\t\tand $\\delta \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Let $\\{A^x_{a,b}\\}$ and $\\{D^x_{a,c}\\}$\n   470\t\tbe POVMs on the right register and $\\{B^x_{a,b,c}\\}$ a projective\n   471\t\tmeasurement on the left register, with marginals\n   472\t\t$B^x_{a,b}=\\sum_c B^x_{a,b,c}$ and $B^x_{a,c}=\\sum_b B^x_{a,b,c}$.\n   473\t\tIf\n   474\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   475\t\t\t\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}_{x\\sim\\mu}\\sum_{a,b}\n   476\t\t\t\\big\\|(B^x_{a,b}\\ot\\Id-\\Id\\ot A^x_{a,b})\\ket{\\psi}\\big\\|^2\n   477\t\t\t&\\le \\delta,\\\\\n   478\t\t\t\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}_{x\\sim\\mu}\\sum_{a,c}\n   479\t\t\t\\big\\|(B^x_{a,c}\\ot\\Id-\\Id\\ot D^x_{a,c})\\ket{\\psi}\\big\\|^2\n   480\t\t\t&\\le \\delta,\n   481\t\t\\end{align*}\n   482\t\tthen\n   483\t\t\\[\n   484\t\t\t\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}_{x\\sim\\mu}\\sum_{a,b,c}\n   485\t\t\t\\big\\|(\\Id\\ot[A^x_{a,b},D^x_{a,c}])\\ket{\\psi}\\big\\|^2\n   486\t\t\t\\le C_0\\delta.\n   487\t\t\\]\n   488\t\\end{lemma}\n   489\t\\begin{proof}\n   490\t\t\\leanok\n   491\t\t\\uses{fact:add-a-proj, fact:triangle,\n   492\t\t\tlem:distance-left-fiber-contraction,\n   493\t\t\tlem:distance-right-fiber-contraction,\n   494\t\t\tlem:distance-joint-marginal-product,\n   495\t\t\tlem:distance-joint-marginal-product-rev,\n   496\t\t\tlem:distance-opposite-placements-commute, lem:distance-symmetry}\n   497\t\tThe argument of Lemma~\\ref{lem:commutation-analysis} with the tensor\n   498\t\tplacements exchanged compares each ordered product to the left-placed\n   499\t\tjoint measurement. The triangle inequality and subtraction give the\n   500\t\tassertion with $C_0=16$; no symmetry of the state is required.\n   501\t\\end{proof}\n   502\t\n   503\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) leaves the\n   504\t% set Y unquantified (finiteness and nonemptiness are needed for the uniform sampling of\n   505\t% y) and places no finiteness condition on the targets R_i (needed for the post-processed\n   506\t% families below to fall under def:bracket); both hypotheses are made explicit here. The\n   507\t% NEEXP original (references/neexp-paper/05_quantum_preliminaries.tex:952-973, label\n   508\t% fact:low-degree-sandwich) instead samples the evaluation point from a specified\n   509\t% question distribution.\n   510\t\\begin{lemma}[Sandwiched simultaneous measurement; modified from Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:ld-sandwich}\n   511\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.sandwichProduct}\n   512\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_sandwich_le}\n   513\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.consistencyDefect_sandwich_le}\n   514\t\t\\leanok\n   515\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   516\t\tLet $k \\ge 0$ be an integer and let $\\eps > 0$. Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution over $\\mathcal X$, and let $\\mathcal Y$ be a finite nonempty set; all statements below that sample $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ refer to the uniform distribution on $\\mathcal Y$. For each $1 \\le i \\le k$ let $\\mathcal R_i$ be a finite set, let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a finite set of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y \\to \\mathcal R_i$, and for each $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be a projective measurement. Suppose that for all $i \\in \\{1,\\dots,k\\}$ the set $\\mathcal G_i$ satisfies the following property: for any two $g_i \\ne g_i' \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the probability that $g_i(y) = g_i'(y)$ over a uniformly random $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ is at most $\\eps$.\n   517\t\tIndex the factors by $0,\\ldots,k-1$; factor $r$ corresponds to the paper's\n   518\t\tfactor $r+1$.\n   519\t\n   520\t\tLet $\\bigl\\{ A^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} \\bigr\\}$ be a projective measurement with outcomes $(g_1,\\dots,g_k) \\in \\mathcal G_1 \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathcal G_k$. Here $\\operatorname{eval}_y$ denotes the evaluation map $g \\mapsto g(y)$, so that $A^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot)_i = a_i]}$ post-processes the outcome tuple by evaluating its $i$-th component at $y$; since the outcome sets $\\mathcal G_1 \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathcal G_k$ and $\\mathcal G_i$ and the targets $\\mathcal R_i$ are finite, these are post-processings in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}. For each $1 \\le i \\le k$, suppose that on average over $x \\sim \\mu$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ sampled uniformly at random,\n   521\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:ld-sandwich-1}\n   522\t\t\tA^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot)_i = a_i]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\delta} \\Id_A \\ot G^{i,\\,x}_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = a_i]}\\,.\n   523\t\t\\end{equation}\n   524\t\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) prints the\n   525\t\t% transposed pairing G^{k,x}_{g_1} ... G^{1,x}_{g_k} ... G^{k,x}_{g_1}, which is undefined\n   526\t\t% when the outcome sets differ; the pairing below is the one of Fact 4.34 in the NEEXP\n   527\t\t% source. The empty product for k = 0 is also made explicit here. See\n   528\t\t% rem:ld-sandwich-indexing.\n   529\t\tDefine the POVM family $\\{C^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k}\\}$, for $x \\in \\mathcal X$, by\n   530\t\t\\[\n   531\t\t\tC^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} = G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{2,\\,x}_{g_2}\\; G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1}\\; G^{2,\\,x}_{g_2} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}\\,;\n   532\t\t\\]\n   533\t\tfor $k = 0$ the empty product is understood as the identity, so that $C^x$ is the measurement whose single outcome, the empty tuple, carries the operator $\\Id$. Then on average over $x \\sim \\mu$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ sampled uniformly at random,\n   534\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:ld-sandwich-2}\n   535\t\t\tA^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = (a_1,\\,a_2,\\,\\dots,\\,a_k)]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{k(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot C^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = (a_1,\\,a_2,\\,\\dots,\\,a_k)]}\\,.\n   536\t\t\\end{equation}\n   537\t\\end{lemma}\n   538\t\\begin{proof}\n   539\t\t\\leanok\n   540\t\t\\uses{fact:agreement, lem:agreement-projective-left,\n   541\t\t\tlem:ld-sandwich-measurement, lem:sandwich-codeword-defect,\n   542\t\t\tlem:sandwich-random-postprocess,\n   543\t\t\tlem:sandwich-root-distance-triangle,\n   544\t\t\tlem:sandwich-postprocess-projective,\n   545\t\t\tlem:sandwich-postprocess-compose,\n   546\t\t\tlem:sandwich-injective-postprocess,\n   547\t\t\tlem:sandwich-matched-tensor-sum,\n   548\t\t\tlem:sandwich-projective-multiplier-sum,\n   549\t\t\tlem:sandwich-tensor-projector,\n   550\t\t\tlem:sandwich-pair-fiber-sum,\n   551\t\t\tlem:sandwich-postprocess-effect-right,\n   552\t\t\tlem:sandwich-joint-marginal-product,\n   553\t\t\tlem:sandwich-fiber-contraction}\n   554\t\tThe source gives this argument by reference to\n   555\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. First apply\n   556\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:sandwich-codeword-defect} to each coordinate: the evaluated\n   557\t\tconsistency bound $\\delta$ and the collision bound $\\eps$ give consistency\n   558\t\tof the full codeword outcomes with error $\\delta+\\eps$. Fact~\\ref{fact:agreement}\n   559\t\tthen bounds the squared operator-family distance for each coordinate by\n   560\t\t$2(\\delta+\\eps)$.\n   561\t\n   562\t\tConstruct the POVM $C^x$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich-measurement}. Induct\n   563\t\ton the number of coordinates in its palindromic product. At an induction\n   564\t\tstep, combine the last coordinate marginal of $A^x$ with the marginal on the\n   565\t\tpreceding tuple. Their product is the corresponding joint projective effect\n   566\t\tby Lemma~\\ref{lem:sandwich-joint-marginal-product}. Replace the two outer\n   567\t\tlast-coordinate effects and the inner prefix in turn. The square-sum bounds\n   568\t\tfor the matched tensors, projective multipliers, and joint-measurement fibers,\n   569\t\ttogether with Lemma~\\ref{lem:sandwich-fiber-contraction}, make these\n   570\t\treplacements contractions. The square-root triangle inequality therefore\n   571\t\tgives\n   572\t\t\\[\n   573\t\t\t\\sqrt{D_\\psi(A,C)}\n   574\t\t\t\\leq 2k\\sqrt{2(\\delta+\\eps)}\n   575\t\t\t\\leq 4k\\sqrt{\\delta+\\eps}.\n   576\t\t\\]\n   577\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:agreement-projective-left} converts this distance estimate\n   578\t\tback to consistency, with a further factor at most two. Thus the explicit\n   579\t\tuniversal choice $C_0=8$ gives the bound before evaluation. Finally apply\n   580\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:sandwich-random-postprocess} to evaluate the complete outcome\n   581\t\ttuple at the independent uniform point $y$; this cannot increase the defect\n   582\t\tand yields \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2}.\n   583\t\\end{proof}\n   584\t\n   585\t\\begin{remark}[Index pairing in the sandwich]\\label{rem:ld-sandwich-indexing}\n   586\t\tAs printed in the source, the sandwich in Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich} pairs the $i$-th measurement with the outcome $g_{k+1-i}$: the outermost factor $G^{k,\\,x}$ carries the outcome $g_1$ and the innermost factor $G^{1,\\,x}$ carries $g_k$. Since the measurement $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}$ is indexed by $g \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the operator $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_1}$ is undefined whenever $\\mathcal G_1 \\ne \\mathcal G_k$, so the printed pairing is a typographical slip. The statement above instead uses the pairing $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}$ of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, from which the lemma is imported. The source also leaves the case $k = 0$ implicit; with the empty-product convention recorded in the statement, both sides of \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2} are then the identity on the single empty-tuple outcome and the conclusion holds with error zero.  The correction is documented in \\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-sandwich-indexing}.\n   587\t\\end{remark}\n   588\t\n   589\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   590\t\t\\label{lem:ld-sandwich-measurement}\n   591\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.sandwichProduct_isMeasurement}\n   592\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.sandwichProduct_isMeasurement}\n   593\t\t\\leanok\n   594\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   595\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n   596\t\tisolates the assertion, left implicit in Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}, that\n   597\t\tthe sandwiched family is a POVM. If $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$\n   598\t\tis a projective measurement for every $i$ and every $x$, then every\n   599\t\toperator $C^x_{g_1,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k}$ is positive and\n   600\t\t$\\sum_{g_1,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} C^x_{g_1,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} = \\Id$.\n   601\t\\end{lemma}\n   602\t\\begin{proof}\n   603\t\t\\leanok \\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n   604\t\tInduct on $k$. Positivity is preserved by conjugation, and summing the\n   605\t\tinner indices gives $\\Id$ by the inductive hypothesis, after which the\n   606\t\tremaining outer pair collapses by idempotence to\n   607\t\t$\\sum_{g_k} G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} = \\Id$.\n   608\t\\end{proof}\n   609\t\n   610\t\\paragraph{Formalization support for the sandwich estimate.}\n   611\tThe following subordinate results isolate the finite-distribution,\n   612\tpostprocessing, and operator-contraction steps used to formalize\n   613\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. They are not named statements of either source paper.\n   614\t\n   615\t\\begin{definition}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   616\t\t\\label{def:sandwich-ordered-coordinate-product}\n   617\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.orderedCoordinateProduct}\n   618\t\t\\leanok\n   619\t\tThis formalization-only definition is the recursive form of the product in\n   620\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The empty product is $\\Id$, the one-coordinate\n   621\t\tproduct is $G^1_{g_1}$, and adjoining the last coordinate places its effect\n   622\t\ton both sides of the preceding product.\n   623\t\\end{definition}\n   624\t\n   625\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   626\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-product-average}\n   627\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.avgOver_distribution_prod}\n   628\t\t\\leanok\n   629\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the iterated expectation used in\n   630\t\tthe evaluation step of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For finite distributions\n   631\t\t$\\mu,\\nu$ and a real-valued function $f$,\n   632\t\t\\[\n   633\t\t\t\\mathbb E_{(x,y)\\sim\\mu\\times\\nu}f(x,y)\n   634\t\t\t=\\mathbb E_{x\\sim\\mu}\\mathbb E_{y\\sim\\nu}f(x,y).\n   635\t\t\\]\n   636\t\\end{lemma}\n   637\t\\begin{proof}\n   638\t\t\\leanok\n   639\t\tExpand the product distribution and rearrange its finite double sum.\n   640\t\\end{proof}\n   641\t\n   642\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   643\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-diagonal-postprocess}\n   644\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.diagonal_postprocess_stateQForm_eq_pair_sum}\n   645\t\t\\leanok\n   646\t\t\\uses{def:distance-state-quadratic-form, def:bracket}\n   647\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the relabeled diagonal overlap in\n   648\t\tthe first step of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If two measurements $A,B$ have\n   649\t\tcommon outcome set $\\mathcal A$ and $f\\colon\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal B$, then\n   650\t\t\\[\n   651\t\t\t\\sum_b q_\\psi(A_{[f=b]}\\otimes B_{[f=b]})\n   652\t\t\t=\\sum_{a,a':\\,f(a)=f(a')}q_\\psi(A_a\\otimes B_{a'}).\n   653\t\t\\]\n   654\t\\end{lemma}\n   655\t\\begin{proof}\n   656\t\t\\leanok\n   657\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-tensor-sum-left,\n   658\t\t\tlem:distance-tensor-sum-right, lem:distance-qform-finset-sum}\n   659\t\tExpand both postprocessed effects, distribute the tensor product and\n   660\t\tquadratic form over the finite sums, and collect pairs with equal labels.\n   661\t\\end{proof}\n   662\t\n   663\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   664\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-placed-defect-expansion}\n   665\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.consistencyDefect_placed_eq_avg_point}\n   666\t\t\\leanok\n   667\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:distance-state-quadratic-form}\n   668\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the pointwise off-diagonal\n   669\t\texpansion used in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For local measurement families\n   670\t\t$A^x,B^x$, their consistency defect is\n   671\t\t\\[\n   672\t\t\t\\mathbb E_x\\sum_{a\\ne a'}q_\\psi(A^x_a\\otimes B^x_{a'}).\n   673\t\t\\]\n   674\t\\end{lemma}\n   675\t\\begin{proof}\n   676\t\t\\leanok\n   677\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-consistency-term,\n   678\t\t\tlem:distance-placed-product-qform}\n   679\t\tExpand the defect and identify each tensor-placed matrix-vector term with\n   680\t\tthe corresponding state quadratic form.\n   681\t\\end{proof}\n   682\t\n   683\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   684\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-evaluated-diagonal-overlap}\n   685\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.avg_diagonal_postprocess_stateQForm_le}\n   686\t\t\\leanok\n   687\t\t\\uses{def:distance-state-quadratic-form, def:bracket}\n   688\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the collision estimate in\n   689\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For a unit state, if distinct outcomes collide under\n   690\t\ta random evaluation with probability at most $\\eps$, then the averaged\n   691\t\tdiagonal overlap after evaluation is at most the original diagonal overlap\n   692\t\tplus $\\eps$.\n   693\t\\end{lemma}\n   694\t\\begin{proof}\n   695\t\t\\leanok\n   696\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-diagonal-postprocess,\n   697\t\t\tlem:distance-qform-nonnegative, lem:distance-point-defect,\n   698\t\t\tlem:distance-placed-product-qform}\n   699\t\tSplit equal and unequal original outcomes. The equal terms give the original\n   700\t\tdiagonal overlap; collision bounds each unequal term by $\\eps$, and their\n   701\t\tnonnegative total is at most one.\n   702\t\\end{proof}\n   703\t\n   704\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   705\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-point-codeword-defect}\n   706\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.point_codeword_defect_le_avg_evaluated_add}\n   707\t\t\\leanok\n   708\t\t\\uses{def:distance-state-quadratic-form}\n   709\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the complementary form of the\n   710\t\tcollision estimate in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Let $A$ and $B$ be\n   711\t\tmeasurements with codeword outcome set $\\Gamma$, let $\\ket\\psi$ be a unit\n   712\t\tstate, and let $\\eps\\geq 0$. If the evaluation map satisfies\n   713\t\t\\[\n   714\t\t\t\\Pr_{y}\\big[\\operatorname{eval}(g,y)\n   715\t\t\t\t=\\operatorname{eval}(g',y)\\big]\\leq\\eps\n   716\t\t\t\\qquad(g\\neq g'),\n   717\t\t\\]\n   718\t\tthen the off-diagonal overlap of $A$ and $B$ is at most the averaged\n   719\t\toff-diagonal overlap of their evaluated measurements plus $\\eps$.\n   720\t\\end{lemma}\n   721\t\\begin{proof}\n   722\t\t\\leanok\n   723\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-evaluated-diagonal-overlap,\n   724\t\t\tlem:distance-point-defect, lem:distance-placed-product-qform}\n   725\t\tExpress each off-diagonal overlap as the complement of its diagonal overlap\n   726\t\tand apply Lemma~\\ref{lem:sandwich-evaluated-diagonal-overlap}.\n   727\t\\end{proof}\n   728\t\n   729\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   730\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-codeword-defect}\n   731\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.consistencyDefect_codewords_le_evaluated_add}\n   732\t\t\\leanok\n   733\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket}\n   734\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary gives the quantitative first step of\n   735\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If distinct codewords collide under random evaluation\n   736\t\twith probability at most $\\eps$, then their full-outcome consistency defect\n   737\t\tis at most their evaluated consistency defect plus $\\eps$.\n   738\t\\end{lemma}\n   739\t\\begin{proof}\n   740\t\t\\leanok\n   741\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-product-average,\n   742\t\t\tlem:sandwich-placed-defect-expansion,\n   743\t\t\tlem:sandwich-point-codeword-defect}\n   744\t\tApply the pointwise codeword-defect estimate and average first over the\n   745\t\tevaluation point and then over the original question.\n   746\t\\end{proof}\n   747\t\n   748\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   749\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-random-postprocess}\n   750\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.consistencyDefect_prod_postprocess_le}\n   751\t\t\\leanok\n   752\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket}\n   753\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the last data-processing step of\n   754\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Applying the same relabeling to both measurement\n   755\t\tfamilies, with the relabeling chosen by an independent finite sample, cannot\n   756\t\tincrease their consistency defect.\n   757\t\\end{lemma}\n   758\t\\begin{proof}\n   759\t\t\\leanok\n   760\t\t\\uses{fact:data-processing, lem:sandwich-product-average,\n   761\t\t\tlem:sandwich-placed-defect-expansion}\n   762\t\tApply data processing for each fixed relabeling and average the resulting\n   763\t\tinequality over the independent sample.\n   764\t\\end{proof}\n   765\t\n   766\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   767\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-root-distance-triangle}\n   768\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.sqrt_opFamilyDistSq_triangle}\n   769\t\t\\leanok\n   770\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   771\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the triangle inequality used for\n   772\t\tthe repeated replacements in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For three operator\n   773\t\tfamilies $A,B,C$,\n   774\t\t\\[\n   775\t\t\t\\sqrt{D_\\psi(A,C)}\n   776\t\t\t\\leq\\sqrt{D_\\psi(A,B)}+\\sqrt{D_\\psi(B,C)},\n   777\t\t\\]\n   778\t\twhere $D_\\psi$ is the explicit averaged squared distance underlying\n   779\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\n   780\t\\end{lemma}\n   781\t\\begin{proof}\n   782\t\t\\leanok\n   783\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-nonnegative}\n   784\t\tFor each outcome write $(A-C)\\ket\\psi=(A-B)\\ket\\psi+(B-C)\\ket\\psi$,\n   785\t\tapply Cauchy--Schwarz to the averaged cross term, and take square roots.\n   786\t\\end{proof}\n   787\t\n   788\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   789\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-postprocess-projective}\n   790\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.postprocess_isProjective}\n   791\t\t\\leanok\n   792\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:bracket}\n   793\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates a projectivity fact used in the\n   794\t\tpalindromic construction of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Every postprocessing of a\n   795\t\tprojective measurement is projective.\n   796\t\\end{lemma}\n   797\t\\begin{proof}\n   798\t\t\\leanok\n   799\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-orthogonality}\n   800\t\tSum the original orthogonal projectors in each fiber of the outcome map;\n   801\t\ttheir cross products vanish and their sum is self-adjoint.\n   802\t\\end{proof}\n   803\t\n   804\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   805\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-postprocess-compose}\n   806\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.postprocess_postprocess_effect}\n   807\t\t\\leanok\n   808\t\t\\uses{def:bracket}\n   809\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates an outcome identity used to align\n   810\t\tthe coordinate marginals in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Successive\n   811\t\tpostprocessings by $f$ and $g$ have the same effects as postprocessing once\n   812\t\tby $g\\circ f$.\n   813\t\\end{lemma}\n   814\t\\begin{proof}\n   815\t\t\\leanok\n   816\t\tExpand the two fiber sums and retain the unique intermediate label $f(a)$\n   817\t\tfor each original outcome $a$.\n   818\t\\end{proof}\n   819\t\n   820\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   821\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-injective-postprocess}\n   822\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.postprocess_effect_of_injective}\n   823\t\t\\leanok\n   824\t\t\\uses{def:bracket}\n   825\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the reindexing step in the\n   826\t\tpalindromic induction of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If $f$ is injective, then\n   827\t\tthe postprocessed effect at $f(a)$ is exactly the original effect at $a$.\n   828\t\\end{lemma}\n   829\t\\begin{proof}\n   830\t\t\\leanok\n   831\t\tInjectivity makes $a$ the unique member of its postprocessing fiber.\n   832\t\\end{proof}\n   833\t\n   834\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   835\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-povm-square-sum}\n   836\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.measurement_sum_adjoint_mul_le_one}\n   837\t\t\\leanok\n   838\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   839\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates a contraction bound underlying\n   840\t\tthe repeated insertion in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For a POVM $M$,\n   841\t\t\\[\n   842\t\t\t\\sum_a M_a^\\dagger M_a\\leq\\Id.\n   843\t\t\\]\n   844\t\\end{lemma}\n   845\t\\begin{proof}\n   846\t\t\\leanok\n   847\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-le-one,\n   848\t\t\tlem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n   849\t\tEach effect satisfies $M_a^\\dagger M_a=M_a^2\\leq M_a$; sum and use\n   850\t\tcompleteness.\n   851\t\\end{proof}\n   852\t\n   853\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   854\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-matched-tensor-sum}\n   855\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.matched_tensor_sum_le_one}\n   856\t\t\\leanok\n   857\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   858\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates a joint-family contraction used in\n   859\t\tthe insertion step of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For two POVMs $A,B$ with the\n   860\t\tsame outcome set,\n   861\t\t\\[\n   862\t\t\t\\sum_a A_a\\otimes B_a\\leq\\Id.\n   863\t\t\\]\n   864\t\\end{lemma}\n   865\t\\begin{proof}\n   866\t\t\\leanok\n   867\t\tBound the matched sum by the full nonnegative double sum and use completeness\n   868\t\tof both measurements.\n   869\t\\end{proof}\n   870\t\n   871\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   872\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-projective-multiplier-sum}\n   873\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.projective_mul_measurement_sum_adjoint_mul_le_one}\n   874\t\t\\leanok\n   875\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   876\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the outer-factor contraction in\n   877\t\tthe palindromic insertion of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If $P$ is a projector\n   878\t\tand $M$ is a POVM, then\n   879\t\t\\[\n   880\t\t\t\\sum_a(PM_a)^\\dagger(PM_a)\\leq\\Id.\n   881\t\t\\]\n   882\t\\end{lemma}\n   883\t\\begin{proof}\n   884\t\t\\leanok\n   885\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-povm-square-sum}\n   886\t\tUse $P^2=P\\leq\\Id$ inside each conjugation by $M_a$, then apply the POVM\n   887\t\tsquare-sum bound.\n   888\t\\end{proof}\n   889\t\n   890\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   891\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-tensor-projector}\n   892\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.LDT.MakingMeasurementsProjective.isProj_kronecker}\n   893\t\t\\leanok\n   894\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the projectivity assertion used\n   895\t\twhen converting distance back to consistency in\n   896\t\t\\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The tensor product of two projectors is a projector.\n   897\t\\end{lemma}\n   898\t\\begin{proof}\n   899\t\t\\leanok\n   900\t\tTensor the idempotence and self-adjointness identities of the two factors.\n   901\t\\end{proof}\n   902\t\n   903\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   904\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-pair-fiber-sum}\n   905\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.DistanceCalculus.measurement_fiber_sum_adjoint_mul_le_one}\n   906\t\t\\leanok\n   907\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   908\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates a marginal contraction used in the\n   909\t\tjoint-family comparison for \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If $M$ is a measurement\n   910\t\twith pair-valued outcomes, then for each first coordinate $a$,\n   911\t\t\\[\n   912\t\t\t\\sum_b M_{a,b}^\\dagger M_{a,b}\\leq\\Id.\n   913\t\t\\]\n   914\t\\end{lemma}\n   915\t\\begin{proof}\n   916\t\t\\leanok\n   917\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-le-one,\n   918\t\t\tlem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n   919\t\tBound each squared effect by the effect itself, enlarge the fixed-coordinate\n   920\t\tsum to the full outcome sum, and use completeness.\n   921\t\\end{proof}\n   922\t\n   923\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   924\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-postprocess-effect-left}\n   925\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.DistanceCalculus.postprocess_effect_mul_effect}\n   926\t\t\\leanok\n   927\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:bracket}\n   928\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates an orthogonality identity in the\n   929\t\tjoint-family comparison for \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For a projective\n   930\t\tmeasurement $M$,\n   931\t\t\\[\n   932\t\t\tM_{[f=b]}M_a=\\mathbf 1_{f(a)=b}M_a.\n   933\t\t\\]\n   934\t\\end{lemma}\n   935\t\\begin{proof}\n   936\t\t\\leanok\n   937\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-orthogonality}\n   938\t\tExpand the postprocessed effect and use orthogonality of distinct projective\n   939\t\teffects.\n   940\t\\end{proof}\n   941\t\n   942\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   943\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-postprocess-effect-right}\n   944\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.effect_mul_postprocess_effect}\n   945\t\t\\leanok\n   946\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:bracket}\n   947\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the adjoint form of the preceding\n   948\t\tidentity in the proof of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}:\n   949\t\t\\[\n   950\t\t\tM_aM_{[f=b]}=\\mathbf 1_{f(a)=b}M_a.\n   951\t\t\\]\n   952\t\\end{lemma}\n   953\t\\begin{proof}\n   954\t\t\\leanok\n   955\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-postprocess-effect-left,\n   956\t\t\tlem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n   957\t\tTake adjoints in the left-handed identity and use self-adjointness.\n   958\t\\end{proof}\n   959\t\n   960\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   961\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-joint-marginal-product}\n   962\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.postprocess_product_eq_effect}\n   963\t\t\\leanok\n   964\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:bracket}\n   965\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the compatible-marginal identity\n   966\t\tused in the palindromic insertion of \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. If the pair\n   967\t\t$(f,g)$ uniquely identifies $a$, then\n   968\t\t\\[\n   969\t\t\tM_{[f=f(a)]}M_{[g=g(a)]}=M_a\n   970\t\t\\]\n   971\t\tfor every projective measurement $M$.\n   972\t\\end{lemma}\n   973\t\\begin{proof}\n   974\t\t\\leanok\n   975\t\t\\uses{lem:sandwich-postprocess-effect-left}\n   976\t\tExpand one marginal, apply the selection identity to each term, and use the\n   977\t\tuniqueness of the common fiber.\n   978\t\\end{proof}\n   979\t\n   980\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich}]\n   981\t\t\\label{lem:sandwich-fiber-contraction}\n   982\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SandwichProduct.opFamilyDistSq_mul_fiber_le}\n   983\t\t\\leanok\n   984\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   985\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary isolates the contraction applied at each\n   986\t\tpalindromic insertion in \\cite[Fact~4.34]{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Let\n   987\t\t$A^x=\\{A^x_a\\}_a$ and $B^x=\\{B^x_a\\}_a$ be measurement families with a\n   988\t\tcommon outcome set, and suppose that $D_\\psi(A,B)\\leq\\delta$. If the\n   989\t\tmultipliers $S_z^x$ satisfy\n   990\t\t\\[\n   991\t\t\t\\sum_{z:\\,\\operatorname{eval}_x(z)=a}(S_z^x)^\\dagger S_z^x\\leq\\Id\n   992\t\t\\]\n   993\t\tfor every $x,a$, then the reindexed families obey\n   994\t\t\\[\n   995\t\t\tD_\\psi\\big((S_z^xA^x_{\\operatorname{eval}_x(z)})_{x,z},\n   996\t\t\t\t(S_z^xB^x_{\\operatorname{eval}_x(z)})_{x,z}\\big)\\leq\\delta.\n   997\t\t\\]\n   998\t\\end{lemma}\n   999\t\\begin{proof}\n  1000\t\t\\leanok\n  1001\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-sum-contraction}\n  1002\t\tGroup outcomes by their evaluated value and apply the finite-family\n  1003\t\tcontraction estimate on each fiber.\n  1004\t\\end{proof}\n  1005\t\n  1006\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:504-505) quantifies\n  1007\t% the pairwise-agreement hypothesis over every pair (x, y_1), including pairs carrying\n  1008\t% probability zero under D, on which no conditional distribution of y_2 is defined; the\n  1009\t% hypothesis is restated here over the pairs of positive marginal probability, the only\n  1010\t% pairs the sampling in the hypothesis can refer to and the only ones the averaged\n  1011\t% conclusion depends on. The NEEXP original (references/neexp-paper/\n  1012\t% 05_quantum_preliminaries.tex:1000-1008, label fact:low-degree-sandwich-on-steroids)\n  1013\t% conditions on a fixed question tuple with the same restriction left implicit.\n  1014\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting two consistent measurements; Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:pasting}\n  1015\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pastedMeasurement}\n  1016\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error}\n  1017\t\t\\leanok\n  1018\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n  1019\t\tLet $\\mathcal D$ be a distribution on $(x, y_1, y_2) \\in \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1 \\times \\mathcal Y_2$. For $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a collection of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y_i \\to \\mathcal R_i$ and let $\\{(G_i)^x_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be families of measurements such that $\\{(G_2)^x_g\\}_g$ is projective for every $x$. Suppose further that for every pair $(x, y_1)$ of positive probability under the marginal of $\\mathcal D$ on $\\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1$ (the only pairs for which the conditional distribution of $y_2$ under $\\mathcal D$ is defined; pairs of marginal probability zero are subject to no requirement) it holds that for $g_2 \\ne g_2' \\in \\mathcal G_2$ the probability, on average over $y_2$ chosen from $\\mathcal D$ conditioned on $(x, y_1)$, that $g_2(y_2) = g_2'(y_2)$ is at most $\\eta$. Let $\\{A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\}$ be a family of projective measurements with outcomes $(a_1, a_2) \\in \\mathcal R_1 \\times \\mathcal R_2$ such that for $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$,\n  1020\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1}\n  1021\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot (G_i)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_i}(\\cdot)=a_i]}\n  1022\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1023\t\tand\n  1024\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2}\n  1025\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\,,\n  1026\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1027\t\twhere $A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i}$ denotes the marginal on the $i$-th part of the outcome (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-conventions}). Define a family of measurements $\\{J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}\\}$ by\n  1028\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2a}\n  1029\t\t\tJ^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\, (G_1)^x_{g_1}\\, (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\,.\n  1030\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1031\t\tThen there is a\n  1032\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-deltap}\n  1033\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta, \\delta) = \\poly(\\eta, \\delta)\n  1034\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1035\t\tsuch that\n  1036\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-3}\n  1037\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}} \\Id \\ot J^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1,\\, \\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}\\,.\n  1038\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1039\t\tNeither the exponent nor the constant in $\\poly(\\eta, \\delta)$ is made explicit in the source; both are universal constants inherited from the proof of Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The literal product-form convention for this notation is incompatible with the unrestricted error domain in the lemma; a two-dimensional counterexample and the required correction are recorded in \\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}.\n  1040\t\\end{lemma}\n  1041\t\\begin{proof}\n  1042\t\t\\leanok\n  1043\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-one-sided-explicit-error, lem:pasting-coarse-commutator,\n  1044\t\t\tlem:pasting-measurement, lem:pasting-scalar-error, lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n  1045\t\tUse the fixed Schmidt mirror of the second codeword family on Alice's\n  1046\t\tregister. Its coarse consistency defect is at most $2\\delta$ and its fine\n  1047\t\tdefect at most $2\\delta+\\eta$. The distinct-family commutator comparison\n  1048\t\tthen proves the conclusion without a register-exchange assumption.\n  1049\t\tFor the universal coarse-commutator constant $C\\geq1$, one may take\n  1050\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}=(3C+19)(\\eta^{1/4}+\\delta^{1/8})$.\n  1051\t\tOn the unit square use Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-scalar-error}; outside it,\n  1052\t\tthis error is at least $1$, so Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-defect-bound} applies.\n  1053\t\tThus the polynomial notation here uses the additive correction documented\n  1054\t\tin \\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}, not the refuted product contract.\n  1055\t\\end{proof}\n  1056\t\n  1057\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting from the forward comparisons]\n  1058\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons}\n  1059\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error_of_marginal_consistency}\n  1060\t\t\\leanok\n  1061\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n  1062\t\tThis formalization-only strengthening of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting} has the\n  1063\t\tsame conclusion under its projectivity and conditional collision hypotheses\n  1064\t\tand the two forward comparisons~\\eqref{eq:pasting-1}, for a unit state and\n  1065\t\t$\\eta,\\delta\\geq0$. The self-consistency comparison~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2}\n  1066\t\tis not required. The local carrier is common to both players.\n  1067\t\\end{lemma}\n  1068\t\\begin{proof}\n  1069\t\t\\leanok\n  1070\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-one-sided-explicit-error, lem:pasting-coarse-commutator,\n  1071\t\t\tlem:pasting-measurement, lem:pasting-scalar-error, lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n  1072\t\tThe Schmidt-mirror proof uses only the two forward marginal comparisons,\n  1073\t\tso its error estimate proves this stronger assertion directly.\n  1074\t\\end{proof}\n  1075\t\n  1076\t\\begin{definition}[Adjoining fixed basis vectors]\n  1077\t\t\\label{def:pasting-product-state}\n  1078\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.productPaddedState}\n  1079\t\t\\leanok\n  1080\t\tLet $\\mathcal H_A=\\mathbb C^{I_A}$ and $\\mathcal H_B=\\mathbb C^{I_B}$,\n  1081\t\twhere $I_A,I_B$ are finite, and fix $a_0\\in I_A$, $b_0\\in I_B$.\n  1082\t\tFor $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$, define a vector on\n  1083\t\t$(\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B)^{\\otimes2}$ by\n  1084\t\t\\[\n  1085\t\t\t\\Psi_{(i,b),(a,j)}\n  1086\t\t\t=\\mathbf 1_{b=b_0}\\mathbf 1_{a=a_0}\\psi_{i,j}.\n  1087\t\t\\]\n  1088\t\tThis is a formalization-only common-space realization for\n  1089\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n  1090\t\\end{definition}\n  1091\t\n  1092\t\\begin{lemma}[Preservation under adjoining basis vectors]\n  1093\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-product-state-preservation}\n  1094\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.productPaddedState_norm}\n  1095\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.stateQForm_productPaddedState}\n  1096\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Pasting.consistencyDefect_productPaddedState}\n  1097\t\t\\leanok\n  1098\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-product-state, def:consistency}\n  1099\t\tFor the preceding construction, $\\|\\Psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|$. For arbitrary\n  1100\t\toperators $A$ on $\\mathcal H_A$ and $B$ on $\\mathcal H_B$,\n  1101\t\t\\[\n  1102\t\t\t\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\Psi,\n  1103\t\t\t ((A\\otimes\\Id)\\otimes(\\Id\\otimes B))\\Psi\\rangle\n  1104\t\t\t=\\operatorname{Re}\\langle\\psi,(A\\otimes B)\\psi\\rangle.\n  1105\t\t\\]\n  1106\t\tConsequently every averaged off-diagonal consistency defect is preserved\n  1107\t\tby these local placements, for arbitrary operator families and any finite\n  1108\t\tdistribution. Positivity and state normalization are not required.\n  1109\t\\end{lemma}\n  1110\t\\begin{proof}\n  1111\t\t\\leanok\n  1112\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-product-state}\n  1113\t\tExpand the norm and quadratic form in the standard bases. The fixed\n  1114\t\tcoordinates remove two sums. Sum the quadratic-form identity over unequal\n  1115\t\tanswers and average over the question distribution.\n  1116\t\\end{proof}\n  1117\t\n  1118\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting on independent local spaces]\n  1119\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-heterogeneous}\n  1120\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error_heterogeneous}\n  1121\t\t\\leanok\n  1122\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n  1123\t\tThere is a universal function\n  1124\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta,\\delta)=\\poly(\\eta,\\delta)$ for which\n  1125\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons} holds with independent finite\n  1126\t\tlocal spaces $\\mathcal H_A$ and $\\mathcal H_B$. The family $A$ acts on\n  1127\t\t$\\mathcal H_A$, the families $G_1,G_2$ act on $\\mathcal H_B$, and the\n  1128\t\tunit state belongs to $\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$. All conditional\n  1129\t\tcollision, projectivity, and forward comparison hypotheses, and the\n  1130\t\tconclusion~\\eqref{eq:pasting-3}, are retained. This is a\n  1131\t\tformalization-only strengthening of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n  1132\t\\end{lemma}\n  1133\t\\begin{proof}\n  1134\t\t\\leanok\n  1135\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons, lem:pasting-product-state-preservation,\n  1136\t\t\tlem:pasting-tensor-placement}\n  1137\t\tA unit state implies that both local spaces are nonzero. Choose one basis\n  1138\t\tvector in each and adjoin them as in Definition~\\ref{def:pasting-product-state}.\n  1139\t\tThe common local space is $\\mathcal H_A\\otimes\\mathcal H_B$.\n  1140\t\tTensor placement preserves projectivity, postprocessing, and the product\n  1141\t\t$G_2G_1G_2$. Both input defects and the pasted output defect are unchanged.\n  1142\t\tApply Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-forward-comparisons} on this common space.\n  1143\t\\end{proof}\n  1144\t\n  1145\t\\begin{lemma}[Universal bound for placed consistency defects]\n  1146\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n  1147\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_placed_le_one}\n  1148\t\t\\leanok\n  1149\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-conventions}\n  1150\t\tLet $\\mu$ be a probability law on a finite question set, let $A^x_a$ and\n  1151\t\t$B^x_a$ be measurements on the two tensor factors with the same finite\n  1152\t\toutcome set, and let $\\psi$ be a unit vector. Then\n  1153\t\t\\[\n  1154\t\t\t\\E_{x\\sim\\mu}\\sum_{a\\ne b}\n  1155\t\t\t\\langle\\psi|A^x_a\\ot B^x_b|\\psi\\rangle\\leq1.\n  1156\t\t\\]\n  1157\t\tThis is formalization support for the complementary error regime of\n  1158\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}; projectivity is not required.\n  1159\t\\end{lemma}\n  1160\t\\begin{proof}\n  1161\t\t\\leanok\n  1162\t\tCompleteness makes the sum over all outcome pairs equal to $1$.\n  1163\t\tRemoving the nonnegative diagonal terms can only decrease it.\n  1164\t\\end{proof}\n  1165\t\n  1166\t\\begin{lemma}[Scalar comparison for the pasting error]\n  1167\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-scalar-error}\n  1168\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pasting_error_sqrt_le_rpow}\n  1169\t\t\\leanok\n  1170\t\tFor $C\\geq1$ and $0\\leq\\eta,\\delta\\leq1$, set\n  1171\t\t\\[\n  1172\t\t\tK=C\\delta+(2\\sqrt{2\\delta}+2\\delta+2\\sqrt{C\\delta}+C\\delta)\n  1173\t\t\t  +2\\eta+2\\sqrt{2(\\delta+2\\sqrt{2\\delta}+\\eta)}\n  1174\t\t\t  +2\\sqrt{2(\\delta+2\\sqrt{2\\delta})}.\n  1175\t\t\\]\n  1176\t\tThen $2\\delta+\\sqrt K\\leq(3C+19)(\\eta^{1/4}+\\delta^{1/8})$.\n  1177\t\tThis is a formalization-only scalar estimate for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n  1178\t\\end{lemma}\n  1179\t\\begin{proof}\n  1180\t\t\\leanok\n  1181\t\tPut $u=\\delta^{1/8}$ and $v=\\eta^{1/4}$. Both lie in $[0,1]$.\n  1182\t\tBound the successive square roots using these variables and compare\n  1183\t\tthe resulting nonnegative polynomials.\n  1184\t\\end{proof}\n  1185\t\n  1186\t\\begin{lemma}[Paired-entry estimate for the Schmidt mirror]\n  1187\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-schmidt-pair}\n  1188\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidt_pair_mirror_estimate}\n  1189\t\t\\leanok\n  1190\t\tFor $s,t\\geq0$ and $u,v\\in\\mathbb C$,\n  1191\t\t\\[\n  1192\t\t\t(s-t)^2|v|^2 \\leq |tu-sv|^2+|su-tv|^2.\n  1193\t\t\\]\n  1194\t\tThis is a formalization-only auxiliary for the one-sided argument in\n  1195\t\t\\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}, not a named assertion of the paper.\n  1196\t\\end{lemma}\n  1197\t\\begin{proof}\n  1198\t\t\\leanok\n  1199\t\tThe difference between the right and left sides is\n  1200\t\t$(s-t)^2|u|^2+2st|u-v|^2\\geq0$.\n  1201\t\\end{proof}\n  1202\t\n  1203\t\\begin{lemma}[Schmidt coordinates for a square coefficient matrix]\n  1204\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-schmidt-coordinates}\n  1205\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_schmidt_coordinates, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtCoordinates_spec}\n  1206\t\t\\leanok\n  1207\t\tEvery square complex matrix $C$ has a decomposition\n  1208\t\t$C=U\\operatorname{diag}(s_i)V^*$ with unitary $U,V$ and $s_i\\geq0$.\n  1209\t\tZero singular values are allowed. This is a formalization-only construction\n  1210\t\tfor the argument in \\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}.\n  1211\t\\end{lemma}\n  1212\t\\begin{proof}\n  1213\t\t\\leanok\n  1214\t\tDiagonalize $C^*C$, normalize the images of its positive-eigenvalue\n  1215\t\teigenvectors, and complete this orthonormal family on the left. The\n  1216\t\tzero-eigenvalue eigenvectors are annihilated by $C$.\n  1217\t\\end{proof}\n  1218\t\n  1219\t\\begin{definition}[The state-dependent Schmidt mirror]\n  1220\t\t\\label{def:pasting-schmidt-mirror}\n  1221\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtCoefficientMatrix, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtCoordinates, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirrorUnitary, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror}\n  1222\t\t\\leanok\n  1223\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-schmidt-coordinates}\n  1224\t\tFor any bipartite vector $\\psi$, form its coefficient matrix\n  1225\t\t$C_{ij}=\\psi_{ij}$ and choose Schmidt coordinates\n  1226\t\t$C=U\\operatorname{diag}(s_i)V^*$ once for that vector. For a Bob operator\n  1227\t\t$B$, define the Alice operator\n  1228\t\t$\\widetilde B=UV^*B^TVU^*$.\n  1229\t\tThis auxiliary operator does not replace any measurement in the statement\n  1230\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}.\n  1231\t\\end{definition}\n  1232\t\n  1233\t\\begin{lemma}[Schmidt-mirror norm estimate]\n  1234\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-norm}\n  1235\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidt_diagonal_mirror_estimate, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidt_action_norm_sq, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror_norm_sq_le}\n  1236\t\t\\leanok\n  1237\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-schmidt-mirror, lem:pasting-schmidt-pair}\n  1238\t\tFor every bipartite vector $\\psi$ and Hermitian operators $H,B$,\n  1239\t\t\\[\n  1240\t\t\t\\|(\\widetilde B\\ot\\Id-\\Id\\ot B)\\psi\\|^2\n  1241\t\t\t\\leq 2\\|(H\\ot\\Id-\\Id\\ot B)\\psi\\|^2.\n  1242\t\t\\]\n  1243\t\tNeither normalization nor invertibility of the reduced state is needed.\n  1244\t\\end{lemma}\n  1245\t\\begin{proof}\n  1246\t\t\\leanok\n  1247\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-schmidt-coordinates, lem:pasting-schmidt-pair}\n  1248\t\tVectorization identifies the squared Hilbert norm with the sum of squared\n  1249\t\tmoduli of the coefficient-matrix entries. Unitary changes of basis preserve\n  1250\t\tthis sum. In Schmidt coordinates, pair the entries $(i,j)$ and $(j,i)$\n  1251\t\tand apply Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-schmidt-pair}.\n  1252\t\\end{proof}\n  1253\t\n  1254\t\\begin{lemma}[Mirrored measurements]\n  1255\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-measurement}\n  1256\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror_one, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror_mul, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror_adjoint, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirror_posSemidef, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirrorMeasurement, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirrorMeasurement_postprocess, MIPStarRE.QPBT.schmidtMirrorMeasurement_isProjective}\n  1257\t\t\\leanok\n  1258\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-schmidt-mirror, def:povm-conventions}\n  1259\t\tThe mirror is linear, unital, adjoint-preserving, positive, and satisfies\n  1260\t\t$\\widetilde{B_1B_2}=\\widetilde B_2\\widetilde B_1$.\n  1261\t\tIt maps measurements to measurements and projective measurements to\n  1262\t\tprojective measurements, and commutes with every outcome coarse-graining.\n  1263\t\\end{lemma}\n  1264\t\\begin{proof}\n  1265\t\t\\leanok\n  1266\t\tThese properties follow from transposition and unitary conjugation.\n  1267\t\tCompleteness and coarse-graining follow by linearity; idempotence is\n  1268\t\tpreserved despite the reversal of multiplication.\n  1269\t\\end{proof}\n  1270\t\n  1271\t\\begin{lemma}[Cross move with independent opposite-register effects]\n  1272\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-distinct-cross-move}\n  1273\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.abs_cross_move_gap_le_sqrt_distinct_families}\n  1274\t\t\\leanok\n  1275\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-tensor-placement, def:povm-conventions}\n  1276\t\tLet $P,G,H$ be measurements, with $G,H$ indexed by the same codewords,\n  1277\t\tand let $\\psi$ be a unit vector. Then\n  1278\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1279\t\t &\\left|\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_{a,g}\\langle\\psi|\\Id\\ot G_gP_aG_gP_a|\\psi\\rangle\n  1280\t\t   -\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_{g,a}\\langle\\psi|H_g\\ot P_aG_gP_a|\\psi\\rangle\\right|\\\\\n  1281\t\t &\\qquad\\leq\n  1282\t\t \\sqrt{\\sum_g\\|(\\Id\\ot G_g-H_g\\ot\\Id)\\psi\\|^2}.\n  1283\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1284\t\tNo equality between the two codeword families is assumed.\n  1285\t\\end{lemma}\n  1286\t\\begin{proof}\n  1287\t\t\\leanok\n  1288\t\tExpand the difference as an inner product of the moved vectors with the\n  1289\t\tcross vectors. Measurement contraction and Cauchy--Schwarz give the bound.\n  1290\t\\end{proof}\n  1291\t\n  1292\t\\begin{lemma}[Collision bounds for independent opposite-register families]\n  1293\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-distinct-collisions}\n  1294\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_codeword_fine_le_coarse_add_distinct_families, MIPStarRE.QPBT.avgOver_pinched_collision_le_distinct_families}\n  1295\t\t\\leanok\n  1296\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-collision-averaging, def:povm-conventions}\n  1297\t\tLet $G^x,H^x$ be measurements indexed by the same second codewords,\n  1298\t\tlet $P^{x,y_1}$ be a measurement, and let $\\psi$ be a unit vector.\n  1299\t\tAssume a probability law $\\mathcal D$ and the conditional collision bound\n  1300\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, with $\\eta\\geq0$.\n  1301\t\tThe fine consistency defect between $H$ on Alice and $G$ on Bob is at most\n  1302\t\tthe corresponding coarse defect plus $\\eta$. Moreover,\n  1303\t\t\\[\n  1304\t\t \\E_{\\mathcal D}\\sum_{g\\ne h:\\,g(y_2)=h(y_2)}\\sum_a\n  1305\t\t \\langle\\psi|H^x_h\\ot P^{x,y_1}_aG^x_gP^{x,y_1}_a|\\psi\\rangle\n  1306\t\t \\leq\\eta.\n  1307\t\t\\]\n  1308\t\\end{lemma}\n  1309\t\\begin{proof}\n  1310\t\t\\leanok\n  1311\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-collision-averaging}\n  1312\t\tThe summands are nonnegative and have total mass at most one.\n  1313\t\tThey are independent of $y_2$ after conditioning on $(x,y_1)$.\n  1314\t\tApply the conditional collision bound to each off-diagonal codeword pair.\n  1315\t\\end{proof}\n  1316\t\n  1317\t\\begin{lemma}[Averaged Schmidt-mirror consistency]\n  1318\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-consistency}\n  1319\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_schmidtMirror_le}\n  1320\t\t\\leanok\n  1321\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-norm, lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-measurement,\n  1322\t\t\tfact:agreement}\n  1323\t\tFor a finite question distribution, any vector $\\psi$, any measurement\n  1324\t\tfamily $A$, and any projective family $B$, the consistency defect between\n  1325\t\t$\\widetilde B$ on Alice and $B$ on Bob is at most twice that between\n  1326\t\t$A$ on Alice and $B$ on Bob. In particular the second forward comparison\n  1327\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting} gives coarse mirror consistency at most $2\\delta$.\n  1328\t\\end{lemma}\n  1329\t\\begin{proof}\n  1330\t\t\\leanok\n  1331\t\tSum the mirror norm estimate over answers and average over questions.\n  1332\t\tThe mirror and Bob are projective, so their squared distance is twice\n  1333\t\ttheir consistency defect. The predictor's squared distance is at most\n  1334\t\ttwice its consistency defect even without projectivity.\n  1335\t\\end{proof}\n  1336\t\n  1337\t\\begin{lemma}[Fine commutators with an independent opposite-register family]\n  1338\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-distinct-fine-commutator}\n  1339\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pinched_coarse_sub_fine_eq_distinct_families, MIPStarRE.QPBT.commutator_mass_fine_le_distinct_families}\n  1340\t\t\\leanok\n  1341\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-distinct-cross-move, lem:pasting-distinct-collisions,\n  1342\t\t\tlem:pasting-commutator-expansion, lem:pasting-ordered-product-mass}\n  1343\t\tLet $G^x$ be projective Bob measurements, $H^x$ any measurements on Alice\n  1344\t\twith the same codeword labels, and $P^{x,y_1}$ any measurement family.\n  1345\t\tUnder the conditional collision assumption and for a unit vector, suppose\n  1346\t\tthe coarse commutator mass is at most $K_0$, the coarse ordered-product\n  1347\t\tmass is at least $1-m$, and the coarse and fine squared distances between\n  1348\t\t$G$ on Bob and $H$ on Alice are at most $e_c,e_f$ on average. Then\n  1349\t\t\\[\n  1350\t\t K_{\\mathrm{fine}}\\leq K_0+m+2\\eta+2\\sqrt{e_f}+2\\sqrt{e_c}.\n  1351\t\t\\]\n  1352\t\tPointwise, the coarse pinched cross term minus the fine one is exactly\n  1353\t\t\\[\n  1354\t\t \\sum_{g\\ne h:\\,g(y_2)=h(y_2)}\\sum_a\n  1355\t\t \\langle\\psi|H^x_h\\ot P^{x,y_1}_aG^x_gP^{x,y_1}_a|\\psi\\rangle.\n  1356\t\t\\]\n  1357\t\\end{lemma}\n  1358\t\\begin{proof}\n  1359\t\t\\leanok\n  1360\t\tExpand the four commutator terms. The reversed-product masses agree,\n  1361\t\tand the ordered-product masses differ by at most $m$.\n  1362\t\tMove the rightmost effect in each cross term to $H$ on Alice, paying\n  1363\t\t$\\sqrt{e_f}$ and $\\sqrt{e_c}$. The fiber expansion gives the displayed\n  1364\t\tcollision identity, whose averaged right side is at most $\\eta$.\n  1365\t\\end{proof}\n  1366\t\n  1367\t\\begin{lemma}[One-sided explicit pasting estimate]\n  1368\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-one-sided-explicit-error}\n  1369\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_pasted_le_sqrt_one_sided}\n  1370\t\t\\leanok\n  1371\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-schmidt-mirror-consistency, lem:pasting-distinct-collisions,\n  1372\t\t\tlem:pasting-distinct-fine-commutator, lem:pasting-ordered-product-mass-lower,\n  1373\t\t\tlem:pasting-pinched-reduction, lem:pasting-pinched-cauchy-schwarz}\n  1374\t\tIn the setting of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, joint self-consistency may be\n  1375\t\tomitted for this estimate. Assume $0\\leq\\delta\\leq1$ and a coarse\n  1376\t\tcommutator bound $K_0\\leq C\\delta$. Set\n  1377\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1378\t\t m&=2\\sqrt{2\\delta}+2\\delta+2\\sqrt{C\\delta}+C\\delta,\\qquad\n  1379\t\t t=\\delta+2\\sqrt{2\\delta},\\\\\n  1380\t\t K&=C\\delta+m+2\\eta+2\\sqrt{2(t+\\eta)}+2\\sqrt{2t}.\n  1381\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1382\t\tThen the pasted consistency defect is at most $2\\delta+\\sqrt K$.\n  1383\t\\end{lemma}\n  1384\t\\begin{proof}\n  1385\t\t\\leanok\n  1386\t\tThe coarse mirror defect is at most $2\\delta\\leq t$ on the stated\n  1387\t\tinterval, and the fine mirror defect is at most $t+\\eta$.\n  1388\t\tConsistency bounds squared distance by twice the defect. Apply the\n  1389\t\tdistinct-family commutator comparison with the ordered-product mass bound,\n  1390\t\tthen the pinched reduction. No reversed marginal comparison is used.\n  1391\t\\end{proof}\n  1392\t\n  1393\t\\begin{lemma}[Conditional pasting with a register-exchanged comparison]\n  1394\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-of-register-exchange}\n  1395\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error_of_register_exchange}\n  1396\t\t\\leanok\n  1397\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n  1398\t\tAssume the hypotheses of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, and additionally assume\n  1399\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1-sym}\n  1400\t\t\t(G_2)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]} \\ot \\Id\n  1401\t\t\t\\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_2}.\n  1402\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1403\t\tThen its conclusion holds. This is a conditional Lean-only result, not the\n  1404\t\tprinted theorem. The additional comparison holds on a swap-invariant state,\n  1405\t\tbut that property is not an explicit assumption of the source operator lemma.\n  1406\t\tThe discrepancy and the plan to eliminate this restriction from the source\n  1407\t\troute are recorded in \\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}, issue \\#201.\n  1408\t\\end{lemma}\n  1409\t\\begin{proof}\n  1410\t\t\\leanok\n  1411\t\t\\uses{lem:pasting-measurement, lem:pasting-coarse-commutator,\n  1412\t\t\tlem:pasting-explicit-error, lem:pasting-scalar-error, lem:pasting-defect-bound}\n  1413\t\tApply the explicit defect bound and dominate it by the universal additive\n  1414\t\tpolynomial error using Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-scalar-error} on the unit\n  1415\t\tsquare. Outside that square the error is at least $1$, and\n  1416\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-defect-bound} suffices.\n  1417\t\tThe full conditional argument is recorded in\n  1418\t\t\\cite{gap:qpbt_pasting-product-error}.\n  1419\t\\end{proof}\n  1420\t\n  1421\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1422\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-measurement}\n  1423\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pastedMeasurement_isMeasurement}\n  1424\t\t\\leanok\n  1425\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n  1426\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n  1427\t\tisolates the assertion, left implicit in~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2a}, that the\n  1428\t\tpasted family is a POVM. If $\\{(G_1)^x_{g_1}\\}_{g_1}$ is a measurement and\n  1429\t\t$\\{(G_2)^x_{g_2}\\}_{g_2}$ is a projective measurement, then every operator\n  1430\t\t$J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}$ is positive and\n  1431\t\t$\\sum_{g_1,\\,g_2} J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = \\Id$.\n  1432\t\\end{lemma}\n  1433\t\\begin{proof}\n  1434\t\t\\leanok \\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n  1435\t\tPositivity holds because $J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}$ is the conjugation of the\n  1436\t\tpositive operator $(G_1)^x_{g_1}$ by the self-adjoint operator\n  1437\t\t$(G_2)^x_{g_2}$. Summing over $g_1$ first leaves\n  1438\t\t$\\sum_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2}$, which equals\n  1439\t\t$\\sum_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2} = \\Id$ by idempotence and completeness.\n  1440\t\\end{proof}\n  1441\t\n  1442\t\\begin{definition}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1443\t\t\\label{def:pasting-tensor-placement}\n  1444\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.leftPlacement, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.rightPlacement, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.leftPlacement_effect, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.rightPlacement_effect}\n  1445\t\t\\leanok\n  1446\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:tensor-product-strategy}\n  1447\t\tThis formalization-only definition presents a POVM placed on one factor of a\n  1448\t\tbipartite space as a POVM on that space. For a measurement $\\{M_a\\}_a$ on the\n  1449\t\tfirst factor the placed effects are $M_a \\ot \\Id$; for a measurement on the\n  1450\t\tsecond factor they are $\\Id \\ot M_a$. In both cases the effects are positive\n  1451\t\tand sum to the identity of the bipartite space, so the comparisons of\n  1452\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, whose two sides are placed families, are comparisons\n  1453\t\tof measurements and the consistency calculus applies to them.\n  1454\t\\end{definition}\n  1455\t\n  1456\t\\begin{definition}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1457\t\t\\label{def:pasting-alphabet-relabelling}\n  1458\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.congrAlphabet, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.congrAlphabet_effect}\n  1459\t\t\\leanok\n  1460\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n  1461\t\tThis formalization-only definition relabels the outcome set of a POVM along\n  1462\t\ta bijection: the effect at an outcome of the new set is the effect at its\n  1463\t\timage. The relabelled family is a POVM. The\n  1464\t\tcomparisons of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting} are indexed by pairs of answers,\n  1465\t\twhile the commutation estimate they feed is indexed by triples; the\n  1466\t\t relabelling mediates between the two indexings.\n  1467\t\\end{definition}\n  1468\t\n  1469\t\\begin{lemma}[Properties of outcome relabelling; formalization support]\n  1470\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-alphabet-relabelling}\n  1471\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.isProjective_congrAlphabet, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.postprocess_congrAlphabet}\n  1472\t\t\\leanok\n  1473\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-alphabet-relabelling, def:povm-conventions}\n  1474\t\tLet $M$ be a POVM on a finite outcome set $\\alpha$, let\n  1475\t\t$e:\\beta\\to\\alpha$ be a bijection, and write $N_b=M_{e(b)}$ for the\n  1476\t\trelabelled POVM. If $M$ is projective, then $N$ is projective.\n  1477\t\tFor any map $f:\\beta\\to\\gamma$ to a finite set and any $c\\in\\gamma$,\n  1478\t\t\\[\n  1479\t\t\tN_{[f(\\cdot)=c]}=M_{[f(e^{-1}(\\cdot))=c]}.\n  1480\t\t\\]\n  1481\t\\end{lemma}\n  1482\t\\begin{proof}\n  1483\t\t\\leanok\n  1484\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-alphabet-relabelling, def:povm-conventions}\n  1485\t\tEach effect of $N$ is an effect of $M$, so projectivity is preserved.\n  1486\t\tReindexing the sum over $\\{b:f(b)=c\\}$ by $e$ gives the stated\n  1487\t\tpostprocessing identity.\n  1488\t\\end{proof}\n  1489\t\n  1490\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1491\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-tensor-placement}\n  1492\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.isProjective_leftPlacement, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.isProjective_rightPlacement, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.leftPlacement_postprocess, MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.rightPlacement_postprocess}\n  1493\t\t\\leanok\n  1494\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-tensor-placement}\n  1495\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary collects the properties of the placement\n  1496\t\tused in Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}. The placement of a projective measurement is\n  1497\t\tprojective, and the placement of a postprocessed measurement has the same effects as the\n  1498\t\tpostprocessing of the placed measurement.\n  1499\t\\end{lemma}\n  1500\t\\begin{proof}\n  1501\t\t\\leanok\n  1502\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n  1503\t\tProjectivity follows from the multiplicativity of the placement together\n  1504\t\twith the idempotence and self-adjointness of the effects. Compatibility with\n  1505\t\tpostprocessing is the additivity of the tensor placement applied to the sum\n  1506\t\tof the effects over a fibre.\n  1507\t\\end{proof}\n  1508\t\n  1509\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1510\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-codeword-cross-consistency}\n  1511\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_codeword_cross_le}\n  1512\t\t\\leanok\n  1513\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-tensor-placement, fact:triangle-for-simeq, fact:data-processing}\n  1514\t\tIn the setting of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting-of-register-exchange}, write\n  1515\t\t$Q_{a_2} = (G_2)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}$ for the coarse\n  1516\t\tsecond codeword family. Then \\eqref{eq:pasting-1-sym}, \\eqref{eq:pasting-2}\n  1517\t\tand the second comparison in \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} give\n  1518\t\t\\[\n  1519\t\t\tQ_{a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta + 2\\sqrt{2\\delta}} \\Id \\ot Q_{a_2}\\,.\n  1520\t\t\\]\n  1521\t\\end{lemma}\n  1522\t\\begin{proof}\n  1523\t\t\\leanok\n  1524\t\t\\uses{fact:triangle-for-simeq, fact:data-processing}\n  1525\t\tCoarse-graining the self-consistency \\eqref{eq:pasting-2} along the second\n  1526\t\tanswer coordinate gives the consistency of the second answer marginal with\n  1527\t\tits copy on the opposite factor, with the same error. The triangle estimate\n  1528\t\tapplied to \\eqref{eq:pasting-1-sym}, to that marginal and to the second\n  1529\t\tcomparison in \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} yields the assertion.\n  1530\t\\end{proof}\n  1531\t\n  1532\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1533\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-coarse-commutator}\n  1534\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_coarse_commutator_bound}\n  1535\t\t\\leanok\n  1536\t\t\\uses{def:pasting-alphabet-relabelling, lem:commutation-analysis-right,\n  1537\t\t\tfact:agreement, def:povm-distance}\n  1538\t\tIn the setting of Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, write\n  1539\t\t$P_{a_1} = (G_1)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1]}$ and\n  1540\t\t$Q_{a_2} = (G_2)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}$ for the two\n  1541\t\tcoarse codeword families. There is a universal constant $C \\ge 1$,\n  1542\t\tindependent of the finite sets, of the space, of the distribution, of the\n  1543\t\tmeasurements, of the state and of the error, such that the two comparisons\n  1544\t\tin \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} give\n  1545\t\t\\[\n  1546\t\t\t\\Id \\ot [P_{a_1},\\, Q_{a_2}] \\approx_{C \\delta} 0\\,.\n  1547\t\t\\]\n  1548\t\\end{lemma}\n  1549\t\\begin{proof}\n  1550\t\t\\leanok\n  1551\t\t\\uses{lem:commutation-analysis-right, fact:agreement,\n  1552\t\t\tdef:pasting-alphabet-relabelling, lem:pasting-alphabet-relabelling}\n  1553\t\tThe two comparisons bound the consistency of each coarse family with the\n  1554\t\tcorresponding marginal of the joint projective measurement $A$ placed on the\n  1555\t\topposite factor, hence by Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} the state-dependent\n  1556\t\tdistances of those two pairs by $2\\delta$. Relabelling the outcome sets of\n  1557\t\t$P$, of $Q$ and of $A$ so that the two coarse families are the two marginals\n  1558\t\tof a common joint outcome set, the right-register form of\n  1559\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:commutation-analysis-right} applies and gives the assertion with\n  1560\t\ttwice its constant.\n  1561\t\\end{proof}\n  1562\t\n  1563\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n  1564\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-collision-averaging}\n  1565\t\t\\lean{MIPSta